var e=JSON.parse(`[[3101,"Code splitting is a technique that lets you break an application into several files (chunks). It shortens the initial load time, because only the parts that are needed are loaded into the page.","Code splitting in Webpack is used to optimise how an application loads. Instead of one large file, several chunks are created, which can be loaded as they are needed. Webpack supports several ways of splitting code:\\nMultiple entry points:\\nSplitting at the configuration level:\\n\\nDynamic imports:\\nLoading modules on demand:\\n\\nOptimising shared dependencies:\\nUsing \`SplitChunksPlugin\` to extract shared modules into a separate file:\\n\\nCode splitting helps improve how fast the application loads and responds, especially in large projects.","entry: {\\n    main: './src/index.js',\\n    admin: './src/admin.js',\\n},\\noutput: {\\n    filename: '[name].bundle.js', // main.bundle.js and admin.bundle.js\\n},\\n\\nimport('./module.js').then((module) => {\\n    module.default();\\n});\\n\\noptimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n        chunks: 'all',\\n    },\\n},"],[3102,"Tree shaking is the process of removing unused code from the bundle. It works with ES6 modules, using static analysis to exclude \\"dead\\" code.","Tree shaking in Webpack removes the parts of the final code that are not used. This is especially useful for libraries, where only part of the functionality is imported.\\nExample:\\n\\nHere the \`unused\` function is not used and will be removed from the bundle if \`production\` mode is enabled and \`TerserPlugin\` is used.\\nTree shaking only works with ES6 modules (import/export) and requires optimisation:\\n\\nThis reduces the size of the code and speeds up how the application loads.","// utils.js\\nexport function used() {\\n    console.log('Used function');\\n}\\nexport function unused() {\\n    console.log('Unused function');\\n}\\n\\n// index.js\\nimport { used } from './utils';\\nused();\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    mode: 'production', // The mode that enables tree shaking\\n    optimization: {\\n        usedExports: true, // Marks the exports that are used\\n    },\\n};"],[3103,"Webpack processes CSS with loaders such as \`css-loader\` (for handling \`@import\` and \`url()\`) and \`style-loader\` (for injecting CSS into the DOM). In production the CSS can be extracted into a separate file.","The following loaders are used to work with CSS in Webpack:\\n\\n- \`css-loader\`:\\nProcesses CSS files and resolves imports (\`@import\` and \`url()\`).\\n\\n- \`style-loader\`:\\nInjects the CSS into \`<style>\` tags inside the HTML.\\n\\n- \`MiniCssExtractPlugin\`:\\nExtracts the CSS into a separate file (recommended for production).\\n\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nIn development mode you can use \`style-loader\` instead of \`MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader\`. That makes testing style changes faster.","const MiniCssExtractPlugin = require('mini-css-extract-plugin');\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.css$/,\\n        use: [\\n          MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader, // For production\\n          'css-loader', // CSS processing\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    new MiniCssExtractPlugin({\\n     filename: '[name].css',\\n  }),\\n  ],\\n};"],[3104,"Ordinary source maps (\`source-map\`) give an exact mapping between the source code and the generated code, but they slow the build down. Cheap source maps (\`cheap-source-map\`) are faster but less precise, since they ignore column information and code-level transformations (for example in Babel).","Source maps help debug generated code by showing errors in the context of the original source. Webpack supports several kinds of source map, which differ in precision and build speed:\\nOrdinary source maps (\`source-map\`):\\nDetailed maps that include information about lines and columns. They are convenient for precise debugging, but they slow the build process down considerably.\\nCheap source maps (\`cheap-source-map\`):\\nThey shorten the build time by skipping information about columns and transformations (for example Babel). They are used for faster debugging, but can be less precise.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nThe choice of source map type depends on what matters more to you: debugging precision or build speed.","module.exports = {\\n    devtool: 'source-map', // Or 'cheap-source-map'\\n};"],[3105,"\`webpack-merge\` is a library that lets you combine several Webpack configurations into one. It is used to create separate configurations for development, production or other purposes, in order to avoid duplicating code.","Projects often need the Webpack configuration split into several parts, for example a common one (base), one for development and one for production. The \`webpack-merge\` library helps combine these configurations while keeping the code better organised.\\nExample:\\n\\nThis lets you tune the build flexibly for different scenarios without repeating the shared configuration.","// webpack.common.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    filename: 'bundle.js',\\n    path: __dirname + '/dist',\\n  },\\n};\\n// webpack.dev.js\\nconst { merge } = require('webpack-merge');\\nconst common = require('./webpack.common.js');\\nmodule.exports = merge(common, {\\n  mode: 'development',\\n  devtool: 'inline-source-map',\\n});\\n// webpack.prod.js\\nconst { merge } = require('webpack-merge');\\nconst common = require('./webpack.common.js');\\nmodule.exports = merge(common, {\\n  mode: 'production',\\n});"],[3106,"Webpack processes images through asset modules or loaders such as \`file-loader\` and \`url-loader\`. You can set up automatic copying of images into the build folder, or inline them as base64 data if they are small.","Webpack offers several approaches for working with images:\\nAsset Modules (the recommended way):\\nThey let you work with images without additional loaders:\\n\\nUsing \`file-loader\` (deprecated):\\nIt copies files into the build directory and returns links to them:\\n\\nOptimising images:\\nFor compression you can use plugins, for example \`image-minimizer-webpack-plugin\`:\\n\\nThis approach lets you improve the application's performance by reducing the size of the resources that are downloaded.","module.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n          {\\n            test: /\\\\.(png|jpe?g|gif|svg)$/i,\\n            type: 'asset', // Automatically chooses between copying the file and inlining base64 data\\n          },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n          {\\n            test: /\\\\.(png|jpe?g|gif)$/i,\\n            use: [\\n              {\\n                  loader: 'file-loader',\\n                  options: {\\n                      name: '[path][name].[ext]', // File name configuration\\n                 },\\n              },\\n            ],\\n          },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nconst ImageMinimizerPlugin = require('image-minimizer-webpack-plugin');\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    plugins: [\\n        new ImageMinimizerPlugin({\\n            minimizerOptions: {\\n              plugins: ['mozjpeg', 'pngquant'],\\n            },\\n        }),\\n    ],\\n};"],[3107,"Webpack can process fonts through asset modules or loaders such as \`file-loader\`. That lets you copy font files into the build folder or inline them as base64 if they are small.","To work with fonts in Webpack you use \`asset/resource\` or additional loaders such as \`file-loader\`.\\nAn example configuration using \`asset/resource\`:\\n\\nIf the deprecated \`file-loader\` is used:\\n\\nThis approach ensures fonts are linked correctly, which matters for the application's styling and performance.","module.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n          {\\n            test: /\\\\.(woff|woff2|eot|ttf|otf)$/i,\\n            type: 'asset/resource', // Copies the fonts into the build folder\\n          },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n          {\\n            test: /\\\\.(woff|woff2|eot|ttf|otf)$/i,\\n            use: [\\n              {\\n                  loader: 'file-loader',\\n                  options: {\\n                      name: 'fonts/[name].[hash].[ext]', // Saves the fonts in a separate folder\\n                 },\\n              },\\n            ],\\n          },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};"],[3108,"\`webpack-chunk-hash\` generates unique hashes for chunks based on their contents. This matters for caching: changed files get a new hash, while unchanged ones stay cached, which speeds loading up.","In Webpack, hashing is used to create unique file names, which helps the browser decide whether a cached resource needs to be updated. By default \`hash\` is used, which depends on the whole build. That is not always convenient, though, since any change leads to the hashes of all the files changing.\\n\`webpack-chunk-hash\` generates hashes based on the contents of each individual chunk. For example:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Changing one module does not affect the hashes of the rest.\\n\\n- Efficient use of the browser cache.\\n\\nIn recent versions of Webpack, \`contenthash\` and \`chunkhash\` are built in and remove the need for an external plugin.","const WebpackChunkHash = require('webpack-chunk-hash');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    plugins: [\\n        new WebpackChunkHash(), // Unique hashes for chunks\\n    ],\\n};"],[3109,"\`html-webpack-plugin\` generates HTML files for your application. It links the scripts and styles that Webpack produces automatically, which makes configuration and updates easier.","\`html-webpack-plugin\` is a plugin that creates HTML files and links the resources (JavaScript and CSS) into them automatically. This is especially useful when file names contain hashes, or when you have several entry points.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Linking resources is automated.\\n\\n- Support for several HTML templates.\\n\\n- HTML is generated even when no template is provided.\\n\\nThis removes the need to update the HTML by hand whenever the build's structure changes.","const HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    plugins: [\\n        new HtmlWebpackPlugin({\\n            template: './src/index.html', // The HTML template\\n            filename: 'index.html',      // Name of the output file\\n            inject: 'body',             // Scripts are injected at the end of body\\n        }),\\n    ],\\n};"],[3110,"HMR lets modules be updated in real time without a full page reload. Webpack Dev Server watches for changes, rebuilds only the parts that changed and applies them in the browser over a WebSocket.","Hot Module Replacement (HMR) is a Webpack feature that improves the development process. Instead of reloading the whole page, the changed code is loaded into the browser, which preserves the application's current state (for example data in forms).\\nHMR works over a WebSocket:\\n\\n- Watching for changes: Webpack watches files for changes using \`watch mode\`.\\n\\n- Building and notifying: when a file changes, Webpack rebuilds only that module and sends the updates to the browser.\\n\\n- Applying the changes: the browser applies the changes through the HMR API without reloading the page.\\n\\nConfiguring HMR:\\n\\nAn example of using it with React:\\n\\nHMR speeds the development cycle up, especially for an SPA, but it requires support in the code or the appropriate loaders (for example \`style-loader\` for styles).","module.exports = {\\n    devServer: {\\n        hot: true, // Enables HMR\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nif (module.hot) {\\n    module.hot.accept('./App', () => {\\n        const NextApp = require('./App').default;\\n        ReactDOM.render(<NextApp />, document.getElementById('root'));\\n    });\\n}"],[3111,"For a production build Webpack uses \`production\` mode, which enables optimisations: minification, tree shaking and content hash generation. The configuration includes code splitting and the optimisation of styles and images.","In \`production\` mode Webpack automatically enables optimisations aimed at reducing size and improving performance:\\nJavaScript minification: \`TerserPlugin\` is used to remove unused code and shorten the syntax.\\nTree shaking: removes dead code.\\nCSS optimisation: with \`MiniCssExtractPlugin\` and minifiers such as \`css-minimizer-webpack-plugin\`.\\nCode splitting: using \`SplitChunksPlugin\` to extract shared modules.\\nImage optimisation: compressing graphics through plugins.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nThis produces an optimal production build with the smallest size and the fastest loading.","const MiniCssExtractPlugin = require('mini-css-extract-plugin');\\nconst CssMinimizerPlugin = require('css-minimizer-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    mode: 'production',\\n    output: {\\n        filename: '[name].[contenthash].js', // Content hashes for caching\\n    },\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n            {\\n              test: /\\\\.css$/,\\n              use: [MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader, 'css-loader'],\\n            },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n    optimization: {\\n        minimize: true,\\n        minimizer: [new CssMinimizerPlugin()],\\n        splitChunks: {\\n            chunks: 'all',\\n        },\\n    },\\n    plugins: [\\n        new MiniCssExtractPlugin({ filename: '[name].[contenthash].css' }),\\n    ],\\n};"],[3112,"The core Webpack concepts are: entry points, the output property, loaders, plugins, modules, modes (\`development\`/\`production\`), code splitting, tree shaking and dependency handling.","The core Webpack concepts:\\nEntry point (\`entry\`): determines which file the application's build starts from.\\n\\nOutput property (\`output\`): configures where the generated file is saved.\\n\\nLoaders (\`loaders\`): transform files (CSS, images, TypeScript) into a format Webpack understands.\\n\\nPlugins (\`plugins\`): add extra capabilities (HTML generation, optimisation).\\n\\nModules: Webpack treats every file as a module and links them into dependencies.\\nMode (\`mode\`):\\n\\n- \`development\`: a fast build with source maps.\\n\\n- \`production\`: an optimised build with minification.\\n\\nCode splitting: splits the code into chunks in order to improve performance.\\nTree shaking: removes unused code.\\nDevServer: a tool for local development with HMR.\\nConclusion:\\nThese concepts help you use Webpack effectively when developing both small and large applications.","entry: './src/index.js',\\n\\noutput: {\\n    filename: 'bundle.js',\\n    path: __dirname + '/dist',\\n},\\n\\nrules: [\\n    { test: /\\\\.css$/, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },\\n],\\n\\nplugins: [new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ template: './src/index.html' })],"],[3113,"To reduce the bundle size you can use code minification, tree shaking, lazy loading, code splitting, image optimisation and the exclusion of unnecessary dependencies.","The main strategies for reducing the bundle size:\\nTree shaking: removes unused code from ES6 modules.\\n\\nMinification: minimises the code by removing whitespace and comments and shortening identifiers.\\nWebpack uses \`TerserPlugin\` automatically in \`production\` mode.\\nCode splitting: splits the code into chunks, so that only what is needed is loaded.\\n\\nLazy loading: loads modules only when they are needed.\\n\\nImage optimisation: use compression plugins such as \`image-minimizer-webpack-plugin\`.\\nReplacing large libraries: find lighter alternatives for large dependencies. For example, use \`date-fns\` instead of \`moment.js\`.\\nexclude/include in loaders: limit the areas where files are processed.\\n\\nRemoving dead code: make sure unnecessary dependencies are excluded from the bundle.\\nThese strategies will help reduce the bundle size, improving the application's load time and performance.","optimization: {\\n    usedExports: true,\\n};\\n\\noptimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n        chunks: 'all',\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nimport('./module').then(module => {\\n    module.default();\\n});\\n\\ntest: /\\\\.js$/,\\nexclude: /node_modules/,"],[3114,"Webpack can be configured for different environments using separate configuration files or \`webpack-merge\`. In development, HMR and source maps matter; in production, minification and resource optimisation do.","To configure Webpack for different environments it is convenient to use three separate configuration files: \`webpack.common.js\`, \`webpack.dev.js\` and \`webpack.prod.js\`.\\nThe common configuration (\`webpack.common.js\`):\\nIt holds the basic settings shared by every environment.\\n\\nThe development configuration (\`webpack.dev.js\`):\\nThe emphasis is on speed and convenience.\\n\\nThe production configuration (\`webpack.prod.js\`):\\nThe focus is on optimisation.\\n\\nWith \`webpack-merge\` you can combine configurations depending on the environment. It is a flexible approach that lets you avoid duplicating code.","module.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    filename: '[name].[contenthash].js',\\n    path: __dirname + '/dist',\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      { test: /\\\\.css$/, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nconst { merge } = require('webpack-merge');\\nconst common = require('./webpack.common.js');\\nmodule.exports = merge(common, {\\n  mode: 'development',\\n  devtool: 'inline-source-map',\\n  devServer: {\\n    hot: true,\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nconst { merge } = require('webpack-merge');\\nconst common = require('./webpack.common.js');\\nconst CssMinimizerPlugin = require('css-minimizer-webpack-plugin');\\nmodule.exports = merge(common, {\\n  mode: 'production',\\n  optimization: {\\n    minimize: true,\\n    minimizer: ['...', new CssMinimizerPlugin()],\\n  },\\n});"],[3115,"To improve performance, use caching, split the build into chunks, limit processing with \`include\`/\`exclude\`, apply \`thread-loader\` for parallel processing and optimise the dependency tree.","The key ways of optimising Webpack's performance:\\nCaching:\\nStore intermediate build results in order to speed up later builds.\\n\\nSplitting the build:\\nSplit the code into chunks in order to shorten the build time.\\n\\nLimiting the processed area:\\nReduce the number of files that pass through loaders.\\n\\nParallel processing:\\nUse \`thread-loader\` with loaders, so that files are processed in several threads.\\n\\nDynamic imports: load modules as they are needed.\\nAnalysing the build: use \`webpack-bundle-analyzer\` to study the build's structure and remove unnecessary code.\\n\\nThese methods will help you deal with long builds and growing code size in large applications.","module.exports = {\\n    cache: {\\n        type: 'filesystem', // Filesystem caching\\n    },\\n};\\n\\noptimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n        chunks: 'all',\\n    },\\n};\\n\\ntest: /\\\\.js$/,\\ninclude: /src/,\\nexclude: /node_modules/,\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n   module: {\\n        rules: [\\n            {\\n              test: /\\\\.js$/,\\n              use: ['thread-loader', 'babel-loader'],\\n            },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nconst { BundleAnalyzerPlugin } = require('webpack-bundle-analyzer');\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    plugins: [new BundleAnalyzerPlugin()],\\n};"],[3116,"Module Federation is a technology built into Webpack 5 that lets several independent JavaScript applications import each other's code dynamically at runtime. It is the foundation of the micro-frontend architecture. It lets you break one large application (a monolith) into smaller, autonomous parts that can be developed, deployed and updated independently of each other while still working as a single whole in the user's browser.","Module Federation fundamentally changes the approach to building and organising large front-end projects.\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- There are two kinds of application: the host (the main application) and the remote (the remote application, a microservice).\\n\\n- The remote application is compiled and exposes some of its modules (components, hooks) for external use.\\n\\n- The host application is compiled with a configuration that states where (which URL) and which modules can be loaded from.\\n\\n- At runtime (in the browser) the host application dynamically loads the code it needs from the remote application and uses it as though it were its own module.\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Independent teams: each team can develop its part of the application on its own stack (React, Vue, Angular) and with its own release cycle.\\n\\n- Independent deployments: one part of the application (a microservice) can be updated without redeploying everything else.\\n\\n- A split codebase: applications are no longer monoliths, which makes them easier to understand and maintain.\\n\\n- Shared dependencies: the technology can share common dependencies (for example \`react\`, \`react-dom\`), so that they are not downloaded several times.\\n\\nAn example of use:\\nA host application (a portal site) dynamically loads a basket widget from a completely different application, which a separate team develops and deploys.\\nConclusion:\\nModule Federation is a powerful tool for scaling development and organising the work of several teams on one large product, making a micro-frontend architecture possible.",null],[3117,"SVG and video are optimised in different ways, but the goal is the same — to reduce the weight and the load on the browser. For SVG you simplify paths, remove unnecessary data and use CSS animations. Video is optimised through codecs, resolution and streaming. SVG is often used for simple animations, and video for complex visual scenes. The choice of format directly affects performance.","SVG and video are two popular but fundamentally different approaches to animation, and they are optimised differently.\\nOptimising SVG animations\\nSVG suits icons, illustrations and simple animations well.\\nThe main techniques:\\n\\n- Optimising the SVG structure\\n\\n- removing \`metadata\`, \`title\`, \`desc\`\\n\\n- merging paths\\n\\n- reducing the number of points\\n\\n- Animating through CSS rather than SMIL or JS\\n\\n- \`transform\`\\n\\n- \`opacity\`\\n\\n- \`stroke-dashoffset\`\\n\\n- Minimising the number of animated elements\\n\\n- animating a group instead of each path\\n\\n- avoiding complex filters (\`filter\`, \`blur\`)\\n\\nOptimising video\\nVideo is used when the animation is too complex for SVG.\\nThe main approaches:\\n\\n- Choosing the right codec\\n\\n- H.264 for compatibility\\n\\n- H.265 / VP9 for a smaller size\\n\\n- Lowering the resolution and FPS\\n\\n- 30 FPS instead of 60\\n\\n- the lowest sufficient resolution\\n\\n- Using autoplay without sound\\n\\n- it lets the browser avoid blocking loading\\n\\n- it reduces power consumption\\n\\n- Lazy loading\\n\\n- loading the video only when it is needed\\n\\n- using poster images\\n\\nConclusion\\nSVG suits light, interactive animations, while video suits complex scenes. Maximum performance is achieved when the format is chosen deliberately and optimised for the specific scenario.",".icon {\\n  transition: transform 0.3s ease;\\n}"],[3118,"Optimising assets comes down to reducing their size and loading them only when they are really needed. For images you use compression and modern formats, for video codecs and a lower resolution, and for SVG cleaning up and simplifying the structure. Lazy loading and code splitting matter too. Sensible asset optimisation directly improves Core Web Vitals. It is a basic skill for production front-end work.","Assets often make up the bulk of a front-end application's weight, so optimising them is critical for performance.\\nOptimising images\\nImages are the most frequent source of unnecessary traffic.\\nThe main approaches:\\n\\n- Using modern formats\\n\\n- \`WebP\`\\n\\n- \`AVIF\`\\nThey give a smaller size at the same quality.\\n\\n- Responsive images\\n\\n- different sizes for different screens\\n\\n- loading the lowest resolution that is needed\\n\\n- Compression without loss of quality\\n\\n- removing metadata\\n\\n- lowering the quality to a visually imperceptible level\\n\\nOptimising video\\nVideo is especially heavy, so it needs separate attention:\\n\\n- Choosing a codec\\n\\n- H.264 for compatibility\\n\\n- VP9 / AV1 for a smaller size\\n\\n- Lowering the parameters\\n\\n- resolution\\n\\n- FPS\\n\\n- bitrate\\n\\n- Deferred loading\\n\\n- loading only when it is needed\\n\\n- using \`poster\`\\n\\nOptimising SVG\\nSVGs often look light but can be overloaded:\\n\\n- Cleaning up the structure\\n\\n- removing \`metadata\`\\n\\n- merging paths\\n\\n- Avoiding complex filters\\n\\n- \`filter\`\\n\\n- \`mask\`\\n\\n- \`clipPath\`\\n\\n- Animating through CSS\\n\\n- instead of JavaScript\\n\\nGeneral techniques\\n\\n- lazy loading of assets\\n\\n- code splitting\\n\\n- caching through a CDN\\n\\n- preloading only the critical resources\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimising assets is systematic work: the right format, the smallest size and loading at the right moment. Without it, even well-written code will not save performance.","<img src=\\"image.webp\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"example\\" />"],[3119,"A bundler is a tool that combines a project's source code and resources into files ready for the browser. It can work with modules, optimise code and process different kinds of file. The best-known bundler is Webpack, but Vite, Parcel and Rollup are popular too. The choice of bundler affects development speed and how easy the project is to maintain.","A modern front end rarely consists of a single file, so doing without a bundler is practically impossible.\\nDefinition\\nA bundler is a tool that analyses a project's dependencies and assembles them into an optimised set of files for use in the browser.\\nThe bundler's main jobs\\n\\n- Combining modules\\n\\n- JavaScript, CSS and other resources are assembled into the resulting files\\n\\n- Transforming code\\n\\n- Support for modern syntax through transpilation\\n\\n- Optimisation\\n\\n- Minification\\n\\n- Removing unused code\\n\\n- Working with assets\\n\\n- Images\\n\\n- Fonts\\n\\n- CSS files\\n\\nPopular bundlers\\n\\n- Webpack\\n\\n- Flexible and extensible\\n\\n- Suits complex projects\\n\\n- Vite\\n\\n- A fast dev server\\n\\n- Uses native ES modules\\n\\n- Parcel\\n\\n- Minimal configuration\\n\\n- Good for small projects\\n\\n- Rollup\\n\\n- Often used for libraries\\n\\n- Excellent tree shaking support\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe bundler is a key part of front-end infrastructure, and understanding what it can do matters for making deliberate choices about a project's tools and architecture.",null],[3120,"Webpack bundles the whole project up front, even in development mode. In dev mode Vite does not bundle the project fully but uses the browser's native ES modules. Because of that, Vite starts and refreshes considerably faster. For a production build Vite also uses full bundling.","Webpack and Vite solve the same problem, but they do it in different ways.\\nWebpack's approach\\nWebpack works on the principle of bundling up front.\\n\\n- It analyses every dependency\\n\\n- It assembles them into bundles\\n\\n- It hands the finished result to the browser\\n\\nThat gives flexibility, but it slows down the dev server's start-up and hot updates.\\nVite's approach\\nVite uses the capabilities of modern browsers.\\n\\n- In dev mode the code is served as ES modules\\n\\n- Only the files that are actually requested are built\\n\\n- Updates happen almost instantly\\n\\nFor production Vite does nevertheless perform a full build.\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- Development speed\\n\\n- Vite is faster at start-up and on updates\\n\\n- Configuration\\n\\n- Webpack needs more setup\\n\\n- Vite is simpler out of the box\\n\\n- Ecosystem maturity\\n\\n- Webpack has an enormous number of plugins\\n\\n- Vite is developing actively, but its ecosystem is younger\\n\\nConclusion\\nWebpack suits complex and non-standard cases, while Vite suits fast, convenient development of modern applications.",null],[3121,"In development mode the bundler is responsible for fast updates and convenient debugging. In production mode it focuses on optimisation and performance. These are two different scenarios with different requirements. Configuring the bundler properly lets you strike a balance between development speed and product quality.","The bundler plays a different role at different stages of an application's life cycle.\\nThe effect on development\\nDuring development, speed and convenience matter.\\n\\n- Dev server\\n\\n- Fast project start-up\\n\\n- Automatic page reloading\\n\\n- Hot Module Replacement\\n\\n- Updating modules without a full reload\\n\\n- Source maps\\n\\n- They make debugging the code easier\\n\\nThe effect on the production build\\nIn a production environment the priorities change.\\n\\n- Code minification\\n\\n- Reducing the size of the files\\n\\n- Tree shaking\\n\\n- Removing unused code\\n\\n- Code splitting\\n\\n- Loading only the parts of the application that are needed\\n\\n- Asset optimisation\\n\\n- Compressing images and fonts\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- A poor dev setup slows development down\\n\\n- A poor production setup makes performance worse\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe bundler directly affects both the developer's experience and how fast the application runs for users, so configuring it is a strategically important part of a front-end project.",null],[3122,"Static content is usually stored separately from the application's business logic. It includes images, fonts, icons and compiled CSS and JavaScript files. In development it may be served by the dev server, and in production through a CDN or a web server. That separation makes caching easier and speeds up page loading.","In front-end projects, static resources need a particular approach because of their volume and how often they are used.\\nWhat counts as static content\\nStatic resources usually include:\\n\\n- Images\\n\\n- Fonts\\n\\n- Icons\\n\\n- Compiled CSS and JS files\\n\\nWhere static content is stored\\n\\n- Inside the project\\n\\n- The \`public\` or \`assets\` folders\\n\\n- Used in development and at build time\\n\\n- After the build\\n\\n- The bundler puts the files into the \`dist\` or \`build\` directory\\n\\n- File names often contain a hash for caching\\n\\n- In external storage\\n\\n- A CDN\\n\\n- Object storage\\n\\nWhy a CDN is used\\n\\n- Fast delivery to users\\n\\n- Servers that are geographically close\\n\\n- Efficient caching\\n\\nConclusion\\nOrganising static storage sensibly reduces the load on the server and speeds up how fast the interface loads for the user.",null],[3123,"Dynamic imports and lazy loading let you break the code into parts and load them as they are needed. That reduces the size of the initial bundle and speeds up the first render. The user downloads only the code that is really needed at that moment. As a result, the application becomes faster at start-up.","Modern front-end applications grow quickly, and loading all the code at once becomes inefficient.\\nThe problem with a large bundle\\nIf the whole application is bundled into one file:\\n\\n- the load time grows\\n\\n- the time to parse and execute the JS grows\\n\\n- First Contentful Paint gets worse\\n\\nEven if the user never visits some of the screens, the code for them is downloaded anyway.\\nWhat dynamic imports do\\nA dynamic import (\`import()\`) tells the bundler:\\n\\n- this code can be moved into a separate chunk\\n\\n- load it only when it is needed\\n\\nWebpack or another bundler:\\n\\n- creates a separate file\\n\\n- loads it asynchronously\\n\\nLazy loading in React\\nIn React the usual combination is:\\n\\n- \`React.lazy\`\\n\\n- \`Suspense\`\\n\\nThis gives:\\n\\n- a smaller initial bundle\\n\\n- loading of a page only when you navigate to it\\n\\n- control over the UX through a fallback\\n\\nHow this affects the bundle size\\n\\n- The initial bundle\\n\\n- becomes smaller\\n\\n- loads and executes faster\\n\\n- The total amount of code\\n\\n- does not get smaller\\n\\n- it is simply distributed across chunks\\n\\n- Caching\\n\\n- chunks are cached separately\\n\\n- changes in one part do not invalidate the whole bundle\\n\\nLimitations and pitfalls\\n\\n- chunks that are too small → many network requests\\n\\n- lazy loading inside the critical path → UI delays\\n\\n- not everything makes sense to load lazily (basic navigation, for example)\\n\\nIn short\\nDynamic imports and lazy loading reduce the size of the initial bundle and speed up the first render. It is one of the key performance optimisation tools, but it has to be applied deliberately, without fragmenting the code unnecessarily.","import('./HeavyModule').then(module => {\\n  module.run()\\n})\\n\\nconst SettingsPage = React.lazy(() => import('./SettingsPage'))"],[3124,"Time to Interactive is the time from the start of the page load to the moment the user can interact with it fully. A page counts as interactive when the event handlers are ready and the main thread is not blocked. This metric shows how quickly the application becomes genuinely usable. It matters more than plain load speed. TTI directly affects the user experience.","Loading a page consists not only of displaying content but also of preparing the JavaScript code. That is exactly where the Time to Interactive metric comes in.\\nDefinition\\nTime to Interactive is the moment when:\\n\\n- the main content is displayed\\n\\n- the JavaScript is loaded and executed\\n\\n- the interface responds to user actions without delay\\n\\nWhat TTI is used for\\n\\n- Assessing the real UX\\nWhat matters to the user is not seeing the page but being able to start working with it.\\n\\n- Finding performance problems\\nA long TTI often points to heavy JavaScript or a large bundle.\\n\\n- Comparing optimisations\\nThe metric shows whether the changes actually improved the application's responsiveness.\\n\\nTypical causes of a poor TTI\\n\\n- a large amount of JS\\n\\n- blocking scripts\\n\\n- the absence of code splitting\\n\\nConclusion\\nTime to Interactive reflects the moment when an application becomes genuinely usable. Optimising TTI is a key task for performant front-end applications.",null],[3125,"The bundler directly affects the size and structure of the JavaScript that is loaded into the browser. Its settings determine how much code is downloaded up front and how quickly it executes. The wrong configuration leads to large bundles and a blocked main thread. A well-configured bundler speeds loading up and improves the performance metrics. This is critical for complex applications.","The bundler determines the form in which the code reaches the browser, and therefore directly affects how fast the application loads and how interactive it is.\\nThe main ways a bundler affects performance\\n\\n- The size of the resulting bundle\\nThe bundler decides:\\n\\n- which modules end up in the final file\\n\\n- whether unused parts of the code are removed\\n\\nA large bundle takes longer to download and executes more slowly.\\n\\n- The structure of how code loads\\nThe bundler controls:\\n\\n- whether the code is loaded as a single file\\n\\n- whether it is split into chunks\\n\\nThis directly affects \`Time to Interactive\`.\\n\\n- JavaScript optimisation\\nDuring the build it can perform:\\n\\n- minification\\n\\n- removal of comments\\n\\n- simplification of expressions\\n\\n- The order of execution\\nA badly set-up build can mean heavy code executes too early and blocks the interface.\\n\\nAn example of the effect\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe bundler is not just a build tool but a key factor in a front-end application's performance. Its configuration directly affects load speed and how responsive the interface feels.","// without code splitting\\nimport './heavyFeature'\\n\\n// with lazy loading\\nimport('./heavyFeature')"],[3126,"The bundle size is reduced by removing unused code and splitting up the loading. Code splitting, tree shaking and lazy loading are used. It is also important to keep dependencies under control and avoid heavy libraries. Minification and compression complement these approaches. Together they speed loading up considerably.","Optimising the bundle size is one of the key tasks when developing performant front-end applications.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Tree shaking\\nIt lets you remove the exported parts of modules that are not used.\\nIt works effectively when ES modules are used.\\n\\n- Code splitting\\nThe code is broken into independent chunks that are loaded as they are needed.\\nThis reduces the volume of the initial download.\\n\\n- Lazy loading\\nHeavy parts of the application are loaded only when they are actually used.\\n\\n- Keeping dependencies under control\\n\\n- replacing large libraries with lighter equivalents\\n\\n- importing only the modules that are needed\\n\\n- Minification and compression\\n\\n- removing whitespace and variable names\\n\\n- gzip or brotli on the server\\n\\nSide effects\\n\\n- splitting that is too aggressive can increase the number of requests\\n\\n- the code becomes harder to debug\\n\\nConclusion\\nReducing the bundle is achieved not by one technique but by a combination of strategies. The main goal is to minimise the amount of code the user downloads and executes when the application starts.","const Feature = lazy(() => import('./Feature'))"],[3127,"Webpack configures the build process of a front-end application. The configuration determines how files and dependencies are processed. You can control entry points, output files and loaders. Optimisations and plugins are configured too. That makes the build flexible and extensible.","Webpack acts as the central tool for building front-end code.\\nThe main areas of configuration\\nThrough Webpack you can control the following aspects:\\n\\n- entry — the application's entry points\\n\\n- output — the format and location of the build\\n\\n- loaders — the processing of JS, CSS and images\\n\\n- plugins — extending the build's logic\\n\\nWorking with resources\\nWebpack lets you:\\n\\n- transpile JavaScript\\n\\n- process styles and preprocessors\\n\\n- include images and fonts\\n\\n- optimise the bundle size\\n\\nOptimisation\\nThe things most often configured are:\\n\\n- code splitting\\n\\n- lazy loading\\n\\n- tree shaking\\n\\n- minification\\n\\nEnvironments\\nDifferent configurations are usually used for different environments:\\n\\n- development\\n\\n- production\\n\\n- test\\n\\nIn short\\nThe Webpack configuration determines the whole process of building and optimising a front-end application.",null],[3128,"Minification is reducing the size of code by removing whitespace and comments and shortening names. Obfuscation is making code harder to read. Minification aims at reducing size, obfuscation at hiding the logic.","Minification and obfuscation are different stages of processing code before it is published.\\nMinification\\nDefinition:\\nMinification is the process of reducing a file's size without changing the program's logic.\\nWhat is done:\\n\\n- whitespace is removed\\n\\n- comments are removed\\n\\n- variable names are shortened\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nAfter minification:\\n\\nObfuscation\\nDefinition:\\nObfuscation is making the structure of code more complex in order to make analysis harder.\\nWhat can happen:\\n\\n- renaming variables to meaningless names\\n\\n- complicating the logic\\n\\n- code generation\\n\\nThe difference\\n\\n- Minification is about size\\n\\n- Obfuscation is about readability\\n\\nWhere each is used\\n\\n- Minification — almost always\\n\\n- Obfuscation — when the code needs to be made harder to analyse\\n\\nConclusion\\nMinification is applied to speed loading up, while obfuscation gives partial protection of the application's logic.","function sum(a, b) { return a + b; }\\n\\nfunction a(b,c){return b+c}"],[3129,"Setting up a build covers configuring the bundler, transpilation, style processing and optimisation. The usual tools are Webpack, Vite, Babel, PostCSS and TypeScript. The bundler combines files, optimises the code and prepares the application to run in the browser. The dev server, source maps and bundle splitting are configured too.","Setting up a build is the process of configuring the tools that turn source code into an optimised production bundle.\\nThe main jobs of a build\\n\\n- Combining files (bundling)\\n\\n- Transpiling code\\n\\n- Processing CSS and images\\n\\n- Minification\\n\\n- Bundle splitting\\n\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Bundlers\\n\\n- Webpack\\n\\n- Vite\\n\\n- Parcel\\n\\n- Transpilation\\n\\n- Babel\\n\\n- TypeScript\\n\\n- Working with styles\\n\\n- PostCSS\\n\\n- Sass\\n\\nAn example of a simplified Webpack configuration\\n\\nWhat is configured most often\\n\\n- devServer\\n\\n- alias\\n\\n- code splitting\\n\\n- tree shaking\\n\\nConclusion\\nSetting up a build is necessary for optimising loading, browser compatibility and convenient development.","module.exports = {\\n  entry: \\"./src/index.js\\",\\n  output: { filename: \\"bundle.js\\" }\\n};"],[3130,"One large SPA is a single application in which all the code is built and deployed together. A monorepo is a way of storing the code of several projects in one repository, though they can be built and deployed as separate applications or as one monolith. Module Federation is a Webpack technology that lets parts of an application (modules) be loaded dynamically at runtime from other, independently built and deployed applications, creating the effect of a single application assembled from distributed sources.","Modern web development has several strategies for organising a codebase and building applications, each of which solves different problems of scaling and teamwork.\\n\\nOne large SPA (a monolith)\\n\\nThis is the classic approach: the whole application is one project, one build, one deployment. All the components, pages and libraries are linked at build time. The upsides: simple initial setup, easy refactoring within a single project. The downsides: as it grows the codebase becomes unwieldy, builds slow down, updates require deploying the whole application, and it is hard for teams to work independently.\\n\\nA monorepo\\n\\nA monorepo is the practice of storing the code of several projects or packages in one Git repository. It can hold several independent SPAs, or a set of libraries with shared dependencies. Tools such as Lerna, Nx or Yarn Workspaces help manage dependencies and scripts across packages. The key difference: the build and deployment can be either centralised (everything is built together) or isolated (each package has its own build). A monorepo improves collaboration on shared code, but it does not solve the problem of independent deployment and execution.\\n\\nModule Federation\\n\\nThis is a technology built into Webpack 5 that lets one JavaScript application load code from another application dynamically at runtime. In essence it is an implementation of the micro-frontend concept at the build level. The applications (hosts and remotes) are built and deployed completely independently. A host application can load, for example, a React component or an entire route from another domain. That provides genuine independence for teams and the ability to update parts of a large application step by step.\\n\\nA practical comparison\\n\\n- Coupling: an SPA is tightly coupled at build time. A monorepo is coupled at the level of code and dependencies. Module Federation is loosely coupled at runtime.\\n\\n- Deployment: in an SPA and a monolithic monorepo the whole application is deployed. With Module Federation each micro-frontend is deployed independently.\\n\\n- Bundle size: an SPA can have an enormous initial bundle. Module Federation lets code be loaded on demand from different sources.\\n\\nAn example Module Federation configuration\\n\\nConclusion: Module Federation is worth applying when you need to build a large application whose different parts are worked on by independent teams, with the ability to deploy and update components separately. A monorepo suits centralised management of many packages or projects with shared code better, but without requirements for runtime independence. One SPA remains a good choice for small or medium projects, where the complexity of a distributed architecture is not justified.","// webpack.config.js of the remote application (remote)\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'remote_app',\\n      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',\\n      exposes: {\\n        './Button': './src/components/Button', // We export the component\\n      },\\n      shared: { react: { singleton: true } }, // Shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// webpack.config.js of the host (host)\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: 'host_app',\\n  remotes: {\\n    remote_app: 'remote_app@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n  },\\n  shared: { react: { singleton: true } },\\n});\\n\\n// The code in the host application for loading dynamically\\nconst RemoteButton = React.lazy(() => import('remote_app/Button'));"],[3131,"Shared libraries are packages of common code (components, utilities, types) used by several micro-frontends. They are used correctly through centralised version management, for example in a monorepo with tools such as Lerna or Nx. That lets updates be synchronised and version conflicts avoided. The key principle is that the libraries should be as independent and stable as possible, so that changes in them do not break the micro-frontends.","Shared libraries in a micro-frontend architecture are a mechanism for extracting repeated code into separate packages, which can then be imported by the independent teams developing different parts of the application. This is needed in order to avoid duplicating things such as UI components (buttons, modals), data formatting utilities, API clients, constants or TypeScript type definitions.\\n\\nThe main approaches to organising them\\n\\nThere are several strategies for managing shared libraries:\\n\\n- A monorepo: all the micro-frontends and shared libraries live in one repository. Tools such as Nx, Lerna or Turborepo simplify the management of dependencies, build scripts and versioning inside the repository. Changes to a library are immediately visible to every dependent project, which makes synchronisation easier but requires discipline in order not to break backward compatibility.\\n\\n- Separate npm packages: the libraries are published to a private or public npm registry. Each micro-frontend installs them as ordinary dependencies, specifying a particular version. That gives more isolation, but it requires updating versions by hand in every project and can lead to a situation where different micro-frontends use different major versions of the same library.\\n\\n- Dynamic loading (Module Federation): the Webpack 5 Module Federation technology lets the code of shared libraries be loaded dynamically at runtime from a single source (the host). That reduces bundle sizes and guarantees that every micro-frontend uses one and the same compiled version of the library at runtime.\\n\\nRules of use and best practices\\n\\n- Minimising dependencies: the shared library itself should have as few external dependencies as possible, especially heavy or conflicting ones.\\n\\n- Semantic versioning (SemVer): strict adherence to the SemVer rules (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) for backward compatibility. Changes that break the API belong only in major versions.\\n\\n- Dependency inversion: the library should not depend on the business logic of a particular micro-frontend. It provides abstractions and primitives.\\n\\n- Testing: the library must be covered by unit and integration tests, since breaking it will affect many projects.\\n\\nAn example structure in a monorepo\\n\\nConclusion: shared libraries are worth applying to stable, well-tested code that is genuinely used by several teams. They help maintain a consistent interface and reduce development costs, but they add the complexity of managing dependencies. The optimal approach is a monorepo with automation tools for medium and large projects, where the speed of synchronising changes matters.","// Project structure using Nx\\nmy-org/\\n├── apps/\\n│   ├── mf-auth/          # The \\"Authentication\\" micro-frontend\\n│   └── mf-dashboard/     # The \\"Dashboard\\" micro-frontend\\n└── libs/\\n    └── shared-ui/        # The shared UI library\\n        ├── src/\\n        │   ├── Button.tsx\\n        │   └── index.ts  # Exporting the components\\n        ├── project.json  # Nx configuration\\n        └── jest.config.js\\n\\n// The code of the Button component from shared-ui\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\ntype ButtonProps = {\\n  label: string;\\n  onClick: () => void;\\n};\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onClick}>\\n      {label}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Using it in the mf-auth micro-frontend\\nimport { Button } from '@my-org/shared-ui';\\n\\nconst LoginForm = () => {\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      <Button\\n        label=\\"Sign in\\"\\n        onClick={() => console.log('Clicked')}\\n      />\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n};"],[3132,"Vite is a modern build tool that uses native ES modules in the browser during development, which gives instant server start-up and fast hot module replacement (HMR). Its advantages include exceptionally fast development thanks to not pre-bundling all the code, built-in support for TypeScript, JSX, CSS and other resources, and an optimised production build through Rollup. Vite's limitations follow from its ES-module-oriented architecture: there may be problems with legacy browsers in development, difficulties with some Webpack-specific plugins, and a need to adapt non-standard workflows.","Vite is a build tool and development server created by Evan You (the author of Vue.js) that rethinks the approach to front-end development. Instead of the traditional bundling of all the code during development, Vite uses native ES modules in the browser. That lets the development server start instantly, since it simply serves the source files and the browser itself loads the modules as they are needed.\\n\\nVite's key advantages\\n\\n- Instant server start-up: there is no need to build the whole application; the server starts immediately, whatever the size of the project.\\n\\n- Fast Hot Module Replacement (HMR): modules are updated almost instantly, without reloading the page, which speeds up the development cycle.\\n\\n- An optimised production build: for the final build Vite uses Rollup, which gives an efficient dependency tree, code splitting and other optimisations.\\n\\n- Built-in support for modern technologies: TypeScript, JSX, CSS modules, PostCSS, WebAssembly and other resources work out of the box without complex configuration.\\n\\n- A Rollup-based plugin system: it lets you extend functionality easily using the Rollup ecosystem.\\n\\nAn example of configuring a simple project\\n\\nLimitations and trade-offs\\n\\n- Browser requirements: in development mode, support for native ES modules is required, which rules out old browsers (IE11, for example). For production the code is transpiled.\\n\\n- The plugin ecosystem: not every Webpack plugin is available for Vite, which may require finding alternatives or writing your own adapters.\\n\\n- Complex configurations: for non-standard scenarios (custom resource transformations, for example) the configuration can be less flexible than Webpack's.\\n\\n- A dependency on Node.js: like most modern tools, it requires a Node.js environment.\\n\\nConclusion: Vite suits modern front-end projects ideally, where development speed matters and an ES-module-based stack is used. It is worth choosing for new applications on React, Vue, Svelte or other frameworks where support for legacy browsers in development mode is not required. For projects with heavily customised build processes or a dependency on specific Webpack plugins, additional assessment may be needed.","// vite.config.js\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite';\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [react()],\\n  server: {\\n    port: 3000,\\n    open: true\\n  },\\n  build: {\\n    outDir: 'dist',\\n    sourcemap: true\\n  }\\n});"],[3133,"A custom bundler configuration, Webpack's for example, often leads to problems with build performance, the difficulty of maintaining the configuration and plugin incompatibility. Developers may run into slow builds because of suboptimal settings or duplicated code. Mistakes in the configuration can also cause problems with tree shaking or with loading resources. Maintaining such a configuration becomes harder over time, especially when updating versions of the bundler or its plugins.","Customising a module bundler such as Webpack lets you tune the build process finely to a project's needs, but it carries a number of risks and difficulties. The main problems come from the complexity of the configuration, the need for a deep understanding of how the bundler works, and the constant effort of keeping the settings up to date as the ecosystem changes.\\nTypical problems\\n\\n- Difficulty of maintenance and understanding: large custom configurations become a \\"black box\\" for new members of the team. A lack of documentation makes the problem worse.\\n- Build performance: suboptimal settings (incorrect caching or redundant plugins, for example) can increase the build time sharply, especially in large projects.\\n- Fragility and incompatibility: plugins and loaders can conflict with each other or stop working after a major version of Webpack is updated.\\n- Problems with tree shaking: the wrong configuration can prevent unused code being removed effectively, increasing the final bundle.\\n- Errors in resource processing: wrong settings for processing CSS, images or fonts can lead to runtime errors or to resources being missing in production.\\nAn example of a problematic configuration\\nConsider a fragment of a Webpack configuration in which problems may be hiding:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nTo minimise the risks:\\n\\n- Start from ready-made configurations (\`create-react-app\`, \`@vue/cli\`, for example) and eject only when it is absolutely necessary.\\n- Document the non-obvious parts of the configuration and the reasons for choosing particular plugins.\\n- Update Webpack and the plugins regularly, checking compatibility.\\n- Use bundle analysis tools (\`webpack-bundle-analyzer\`, for example) to keep the size under control.\\n- Write tests for the critical parts of the build process where that is possible.\\nConclusion: a custom bundler configuration is a powerful but risky tool. It is worth applying when the standard tools do not cover the project's specific requirements (special asset processing or custom code splitting, for example). In most cases it is better to rely on proven templates and the smallest changes necessary.","module.exports = {\\n  // ...\\n  optimization: {\\n    // Problem: splitChunks is configured too aggressively,\\n    // which can create many small chunks and make loading worse.\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      chunks: 'all',\\n      minSize: 0 // A dangerous setting – the chunks may be too small.\\n    },\\n    // Problem: minification is disabled for production.\\n    minimize: false\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.js$/,\\n        // Problem: excluding node_modules can break tree shaking\\n        // for some libraries.\\n        exclude: /node_modules/,\\n        use: 'babel-loader'\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    // Problem: several plugins may duplicate functionality\\n    // or conflict with each other.\\n    new SomeCustomPlugin(),\\n    new SomeOtherPlugin()\\n  ]\\n};"],[3134,"Micro-frontends are an architectural style in which the web interface is broken into independent, autonomous parts that can be developed, deployed and scaled by separate teams. The main goal is to overcome the limitations of a monolithic front end in large projects by letting teams work independently with different technologies. The key approaches include build-time composition, server-side integration and client-side integration using tools such as Webpack Module Federation or frameworks like single-spa. This increases development speed and makes large applications easier to maintain.","Micro-frontends are an approach to front-end development inspired by microservice architecture. Instead of one large monolithic application, the interface is broken into small, loosely coupled parts — \\"micro-frontends\\". Each part is responsible for a particular business capability (a product catalogue, a basket, a user profile, for example) and can be developed by a separate team with its own technology stack, build process and deployment cycle.\\n\\nThe main approaches to implementation\\n\\nThere are several strategies for integrating micro-frontends into a single application:\\n\\n- Build-time composition: the individual micro-frontends are published as packages (npm, for example), and the main application includes them as dependencies and builds everything together. This is simple, but it requires rebuilding the whole application whenever any micro-frontend is updated.\\n\\n- Server-side integration: the server (using Nginx or a dedicated composer, for example) assembles HTML fragments from different micro-frontends into a single page. This improves performance, but it adds complexity on the server.\\n\\n- Client-side integration: this is the most popular approach. The main application (the \\"shell\\") dynamically loads and renders the micro-frontends at runtime in the browser. This gives maximum deployment independence.\\n\\nPopular tools and technologies\\n\\nFor client-side integration the common choices are:\\n\\n- Webpack Module Federation: it lets one application load code from another dynamically at runtime. It has become the de facto standard.\\n\\n- single-spa: a meta-framework that coordinates the loading and rendering of micro-frontends written in different technologies (React, Vue, Angular).\\n\\n- Web Components: using native browser standards to create encapsulated components that can be embedded in any application.\\n\\nExample code with Webpack Module Federation\\n\\nThe configuration of the main application (host) and of the remote micro-frontend (remote).\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nMicro-frontends are especially useful in large companies with several front-end teams working on one product (marketplaces, banking applications, corporate portals, for example). They let you speed development up, isolate risks and modernise legacy code gradually.\\n\\nConclusion: a micro-frontend architecture is worth applying to large, long-lived web applications where the independence of teams and the frequency of deployments are critically important factors. For small projects or applications with a single team, the complexity of this architecture may outweigh its advantages.","// webpack.config.js of the main application (host)\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'hostApp',\\n      remotes: {\\n        // We state where to look for the remote module\\n        mfCart: 'cartApp@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'], // Shared dependencies\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// In the main application's code the React component is loaded dynamically\\nconst RemoteCart = React.lazy(() => import('mfCart/CartComponent'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Shop</h1>\\n      <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading…</div>}>\\n        <RemoteCart />\\n      </React.Suspense>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[3135,"Webpack optimises the reuse of libraries first of all by creating a separate vendor chunk. It moves third-party libraries (React, Lodash, for example) out of the application's own code into a separate file. That lets the browser cache this file for a long time, since libraries change rarely. When the application's code is updated, the user needs to download only the changed main chunk, while the libraries are taken from the cache, which speeds up repeat visits.","Webpack uses several strategies to optimise the reuse of libraries, the main one being code splitting. The goal is to separate the rarely changing code of third-party dependencies (libraries) from the frequently changing code of the application itself.\\n\\nCreating a vendor chunk\\n\\nThe most common approach is to configure splitting so that every module from the \`node_modules\` folder ends up in a separate bundle, often called \`vendor.js\` or \`vendors~main.js\`. This is done by configuring an entry point or the \`SplitChunksPlugin\` plugin.\\n\\nHow it works and where it is used\\n\\nDuring the build Webpack analyses the dependency graph. Every module that matches the rule (being inside \`node_modules\`, for example) is placed in a separate chunk. In practice this means libraries such as React, Vue or Lodash will be downloaded once on the first visit to the site and stored in the browser cache. When the application's functionality is updated later (and the main chunk changes), the browser does not need to download those libraries again — they are taken from the cache. This is critically important for large applications and increases load speed for regular users.\\n\\nAdditional optimisations\\n\\n- File name hashing: the names of the output files include a hash of their contents (\`vendors.abc123.js\`, for example). If the libraries have not changed, the hash stays the same and the browser uses the cached version.\\n\\n- Minification and compression: the libraries' code is minified and compressed in order to reduce the amount transferred.\\n\\n- Tree shaking: for ES6 modules Webpack can exclude unused code from libraries where they support it, making the vendor chunk smaller still.\\n\\nConclusion: this approach is worth applying in practically any medium or large project that uses Webpack. It gives an immediate performance gain through long-term caching of libraries, which improves the user experience, especially for returning users.","// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      cacheGroups: {\\n        vendor: {\\n          test: /[\\\\\\\\/]node_modules[\\\\\\\\/]/,\\n          name: 'vendors',\\n          chunks: 'all',\\n        },\\n      },\\n    },\\n  },\\n};"],[3136,"Importing a single function can pull in the whole library if the module bundler (Webpack, for example) cannot safely remove the unused code. That happens when a module has side effects on import, for example changing global variables or executing code. Some libraries also do not support tree shaking because of the structure of their exports (exporting through a single object, for example). To avoid it, use libraries that support ES modules and configure \`sideEffects\` in the bundler's configuration.","When you import a single function from a library, you expect only its code to end up in the resulting bundle. Sometimes, though, the whole library is pulled in, which increases the application's size. This has to do with how module bundlers such as Webpack or Rollup work and their ability to perform \\"tree shaking\\" — removing dead code.\\nHow tree shaking works\\nTree shaking is the process of statically analysing imports and exports in ES modules. The bundler determines which exports are actually used and excludes the unused ones. For that, the code must be written in the ES module format (import/export), since that can be analysed statically.\\nWhy tree shaking may not work\\n\\n- Side effects: if a module performs actions on import (registering polyfills or changing the global object, for example), the bundler cannot remove it, since that could break the application. In the Webpack configuration you need to state \`sideEffects: false\` explicitly for libraries without side effects.\\n- CommonJS modules: libraries exported through \`module.exports\` are loaded dynamically, which makes analysis harder. Bundlers often cannot remove unused code from such modules.\\n- The structure of the exports: if a library exports all its functions through a single object (\`import _ from 'lodash'\`, for example), then even when one function is used the whole module may be pulled in. Modern versions of libraries often support modular imports (\`import debounce from 'lodash/debounce'\`, for example).\\nExample code and configuration\\nConsider an example with a library that does not support tree shaking because of side effects:\\n\\nWhen only \`add\` is imported, the bundler may include the whole module, because there is a side effect. To fix that, the side effects need to be moved into a separate module, or the library's package.json needs to state:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use libraries that support ES modules and tree shaking (the ES version of Lodash, for example).\\n- Configure \`sideEffects\` in the Webpack configuration or in package.json.\\n- Check the bundle size with tools such as Webpack Bundle Analyzer.\\nConclusion: tree shaking is a powerful optimisation method, but it requires a correct code structure and bundler configuration. Apply it to reduce the bundle size in production builds, especially in large applications with many dependencies.","// mathUtils.js\\nexport function add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\nexport function multiply(a, b) {\\n  return a * b;\\n}\\n// A side effect: changing a global variable\\nwindow.myLib = { version: '1.0' };\\n\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"my-lib\\",\\n  \\"sideEffects\\": false\\n}"],[3137,"Bundlers such as Webpack, Vite or Parcel are used to set up a front-end build. The main approaches include code splitting, minification, transpilation through Babel, asset management and configuring a dev server. Loaders and plugins are also used to process various kinds of file. It is important to configure the development and production modes separately.","The main approaches to setting up a front-end build\\nA front-end build is the process of turning source code (JS, CSS, images) into an optimised set of files for the browser. The main tools are Webpack, Vite, Parcel and Rollup. Each of them offers its own approach to configuration.\\nThe key concepts\\n\\n- Entry and output: the entry point and the folder for the bundled files.\\n- Loaders: they process files (babel-loader for JS, css-loader for CSS, for example).\\n- Plugins: they extend functionality (HtmlWebpackPlugin for generating HTML, for example).\\n- Code splitting: splitting the code into chunks for lazy loading.\\n- Module Federation: it lets you load modules from other builds (micro-frontends).\\nAn example Webpack configuration\\n\\nConclusion\\nSetting up a front-end build is necessary for optimising performance, reducing the bundle size and improving the development experience. Use Webpack for complex projects with micro-frontends, Vite for fast development, and Parcel for simple configurations without unnecessary setup.","const path = require('path');\\nconst HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n    filename: '[name].[contenthash].js',\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      { test: /\\\\.js$/, exclude: /node_modules/, use: 'babel-loader' },\\n      { test: /\\\\.css$/, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [new HtmlWebpackPlugin({ template: './src/index.html' })],\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: { chunks: 'all' },\\n  },\\n};"],[3138,"PostCSS is a tool for transforming CSS through plugins. For cross-browser support the Autoprefixer plugin is used, which adds vendor prefixes based on Can I Use data. That spares the developer from writing prefixes by hand and makes supporting older browsers easier.","What PostCSS is and how it works\\nPostCSS is a tool that transforms CSS using plugins written in JavaScript. It is not a preprocessor like Sass or Less but rather a platform for processing styles. The core idea is to parse the CSS into an abstract syntax tree (AST), apply plugins to it and generate new CSS.\\nIts role in cross-browser support\\nThe key plugin for ensuring cross-browser support is Autoprefixer. It analyses the CSS and adds vendor prefixes (\`-webkit-\`, \`-moz-\`, for example) based on data from the Can I Use service. That automates a process which used to be done by hand or with preprocessors.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nOther useful plugins\\n\\n- cssnano — CSS minification for production.\\n- PostCSS Preset Env — it lets you use modern CSS features (\`custom properties\`, for example), converting them into compatible equivalents.\\n- Stylelint — CSS linting for keeping to standards.\\nConclusion\\nPostCSS with Autoprefixer is the standard tool for automating cross-browser support in modern projects. It makes supporting older browsers easier, reduces manual work and integrates into bundlers (Webpack, Gulp). It is recommended in any project where compatibility with different browsers matters.","/* The original CSS */\\n.example {\\n  display: flex;\\n  transition: all 0.3s;\\n}\\n\\n/* After Autoprefixer has processed it */\\n.example {\\n  display: -webkit-box;\\n  display: -ms-flexbox;\\n  display: flex;\\n  -webkit-transition: all 0.3s;\\n  -o-transition: all 0.3s;\\n  transition: all 0.3s;\\n}"],[3139,"Micro-frontends are an architectural approach in which a front-end application is broken into independent, loosely coupled modules. Each module is developed, tested and deployed separately, which lets teams work in parallel. This improves scalability and makes large projects easier to maintain.","What are micro-frontends?\\nMicro-frontends are an architectural style in which a web application's interface is broken into small, independent parts, each responsible for its own functionality. The approach borrows ideas from microservice architecture on the back end but applies them to the front end. The main goal is to avoid a monolithic front end, which is hard to maintain and scale.\\nHow does it work?\\nEach micro-frontend is a separate application, which may be written in a different framework (React, Vue, Angular) and managed by its own team. The integration happens at the browser level using techniques such as iframes, Web Components or dedicated frameworks (Module Federation in Webpack 5, for example).\\nAn example of integration through Module Federation\\n\\nThe main application can load that component dynamically:\\n\\nAdvantages and disadvantages\\n\\n- Advantages: independent development, isolated deployment, technological flexibility, better scalability.\\n- Disadvantages: the complexity of integration, a larger bundle size, the need for a single design system, potential performance problems.\\nConclusion: micro-frontends are worth applying in large projects with several teams, where independence of development and deployment matters. For small applications the approach may be excessive because of the extra complexity.","// webpack.config.js for the micro-frontend\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'app1',\\n      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',\\n      exposes: {\\n        './Header': './src/Header',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// The main application\\nconst Header = React.lazy(() => import('app1/Header'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Header />\\n    </React.Suspense>\\n  );\\n}"],[3140,"Yes — the values of environment variables used in the code end up in the final bundle if they are substituted at build time. That can lead to sensitive data leaking, such as API keys or secrets. So it is important not to keep secrets in client code and to use server-side environment variables instead.","How environment variables end up in the bundle\\nEnvironment variables are values passed to an application at runtime. During the build (with Webpack or Vite, for example) these variables can be substituted directly into the code. If a variable is used in client code, its value will be embedded in the final bundle that is sent to the browser.\\nAn example with Webpack\\nConsider an example using Webpack and the \`DefinePlugin\` plugin:\\n\\nAfter the build, the variable's value will be inserted into the bundle, for example \`https://api.example.com\`. That means any user can see this value by opening the application's source code in the browser.\\nSecurity risks\\nIf the environment variables hold secrets (database access keys or tokens, for example), they become available to everyone who loads your application. So sensitive data should never be passed into client code. Use server-side environment variables for secrets and pass only the data the client needs in order to work (public API keys, for example).\\nConclusion\\nEnvironment variables used in client code end up in the final bundle and become visible to users. That is fine for public data but unacceptable for secrets. For safe operation, use server-side environment variables and pass only the necessary public values.","// webpack.config.js\\nconst webpack = require('webpack');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new webpack.DefinePlugin({\\n      'process.env.API_URL': JSON.stringify(process.env.API_URL)\\n    })\\n  ]\\n};\\n\\n// In the application's code\\nconst apiUrl = process.env.API_URL;\\nconsole.log(apiUrl); // After the build it will be replaced with the actual value"],[3141,"Environment variables are substituted at build time with dedicated tools, for example DefinePlugin in Webpack. They are replaced by their values directly in the code before execution. That lets you use different configurations for different environments without changing the source code.","How the substitution of environment variables at build time works\\nSubstituting environment variables during the build is the process of replacing special markers in the source code with the actual values known at the moment the application is compiled or built. That lets you produce different builds for different environments (development, testing, production) without changing the code itself.\\nThe main mechanisms\\n\\n- Webpack DefinePlugin: it lets you create global constants that are replaced at compile time. For example, \`process.env.NODE_ENV\` can be replaced with \`\\"production\\"\`.\\n- Vite define: a similar mechanism, where the variables are set through the configuration and substituted into the code.\\n- Docker build args: when building a Docker image you can pass arguments that will be used in the Dockerfile.\\nAn example with Webpack\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis approach is widely used for:\\n\\n- Configuring an API URL for different environments.\\n- Enabling or disabling debugging features.\\n- Managing access keys for third-party services (but not for secrets!).\\nIt is important to remember that environment variables substituted at build time become part of the resulting bundle and are visible in the client code. So this mechanism should not be used to store secret data (passwords, tokens). Server-side environment variables are better for that.\\nConclusion: substituting environment variables at build time is a powerful tool for configuring applications for different environments, which increases flexibility and security when it is used correctly.","// webpack.config.js\\nconst webpack = require('webpack');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new webpack.DefinePlugin({\\n      'process.env.API_URL': JSON.stringify('https://api.example.com'),\\n      'process.env.NODE_ENV': JSON.stringify('production')\\n    })\\n  ]\\n};\\n\\n// In the application's code\\nconsole.log(process.env.API_URL); // After the build: 'https://api.example.com'"],[3142,"CSS modules solve the problem of class name collisions by generating unique class names automatically at build time. Every class from a CSS file is turned into a hashed string that is guaranteed to be unique across the whole application. That lets developers use short, clear names without the risk of clashing with other components.","How CSS modules solve the problem of class name collisions\\nCSS modules are an approach in which a component's styles are isolated at build time. The main problem they solve is CSS's global namespace, where two identical classes from different files can conflict. CSS modules automatically generate unique class names by adding a hash or another identifier to them, which rules out collisions.\\nThe principle\\nAt build time (with Webpack and css-loader, for example) each CSS file is processed as a module. Every class name in it is turned into a unique string, and in the JavaScript code they are imported as an object whose key is the original name and whose value is the generated one. That lets you use short names during development while guaranteeing uniqueness at runtime.\\nExample code\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- In React, Vue, Angular and other frameworks, to isolate components' styles.\\n- In projects with a large codebase, where it is important to avoid accidental overrides.\\n- When using CSS preprocessors (Sass, Less) together with modules.\\nConclusion\\nCSS modules are worth applying in any modern web project that uses a component-based approach. They make styles predictable, simplify refactoring and remove the headache of global CSS conflicts, especially in teams.","// styles.module.css\\n.button {\\n  background: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n}\\n\\n// Component.jsx\\nimport styles from './styles.module.css';\\n\\nfunction Button() {\\n  return <button className={styles.button}>Click me</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// In the browser the class will look like \\"_button_1a2b3\\""],[3143,"To add support for a CSS preprocessor, Sass for example, you need to install the relevant loaders: sass-loader, css-loader and style-loader. Then, in the Webpack configuration's \`module.rules\` section, add a rule for \`.scss\` files listing these loaders in right-to-left order. That will let Webpack process Sass files, convert them into CSS and inject them into the HTML.","How to add support for CSS preprocessors in Webpack\\nCSS preprocessors such as Sass, Less or Stylus extend what ordinary CSS can do by adding variables, nesting, mixins and other features. For Webpack to be able to process these preprocessors' files, the relevant loaders have to be configured. Loaders are modules that transform files before they are added to the bundle.\\nInstalling the necessary packages\\nWorking with Sass (the most popular preprocessor) requires three packages:\\n\\n- \`sass-loader\` — compiles Sass/SCSS into CSS.\\n- \`css-loader\` — interprets \`@import\` and \`url()\` in CSS.\\n- \`style-loader\` — injects the CSS into the DOM through a \`<style>\` tag.\\nThey are installed with the command:\\n\\nConfiguring Webpack\\nIn the \`webpack.config.js\` file we add a rule for files with the \`.scss\` or \`.sass\` extension:\\n\\nThe order of the loaders matters: Webpack applies them right to left. First \`sass-loader\` converts the SCSS into CSS, then \`css-loader\` processes the CSS, and finally \`style-loader\` adds the styles to the HTML.\\nAn example of use\\nOnce it is configured you can import SCSS files in JavaScript:\\n\\nWebpack will process the file automatically and add the styles to the page.\\nConclusion\\nSetting up support for CSS preprocessors in Webpack is a standard task for modern front-end projects. It lets you use the preprocessors' powerful features while keeping the build automated. This approach is used in most React, Vue and other SPA applications in order to make working with styles easier.","npm install --save-dev sass-loader sass css-loader style-loader\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.scss$/,\\n        use: [\\n          'style-loader',\\n          'css-loader',\\n          'sass-loader'\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nimport './styles.scss';\\n\\nconst element = document.createElement('div');\\nelement.className = 'container';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(element);"],[3144,"For a small React project with corporate standards it is best to use Webpack, since it is the most flexible and configurable bundler. It supports React out of the box through Babel and lets you integrate any plugins and loaders easily. If the company already has ready-made configurations or templates, Webpack will be compatible with them. Alternatives such as Vite or Parcel may be simpler, but they may not match the internal standards.","Choosing a bundler for a React project\\nWhen choosing a bundler for a small React project under corporate standards, the key factor is compatibility with the existing infrastructure. Webpack is the de facto standard in large companies thanks to its flexibility and its wide plugin ecosystem. It lets you tune the build process precisely to any requirements, including optimisation, code splitting and support for legacy browsers.\\nA practical example of a Webpack configuration for React\\n\\nWhy Webpack rather than the alternatives\\n\\n- Compatibility with standards: Webpack adapts easily to existing corporate configurations, ESLint, Prettier and other tools.\\n- Flexibility: the ability to tune it finely through loaders and plugins for working with TypeScript, CSS modules, images and fonts.\\n- Performance: built-in optimisations such as tree shaking and code splitting, which are critical for production builds.\\nAlthough Vite offers faster development thanks to ESM, it may not support specific corporate plugins or may require extra configuration for compatibility. Parcel, for its part, is too automated and does not give enough control.\\nConclusion\\nFor a small React project in a company with established standards, Webpack remains the optimal choice thanks to its flexibility, maturity and compatibility with existing tools. It gives full control over the build and integrates easily into any corporate environment.","// webpack.config.js\\nconst path = require('path');\\nconst HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n    filename: 'bundle.js',\\n  },\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.jsx?$/,\\n        exclude: /node_modules/,\\n        use: {\\n          loader: 'babel-loader',\\n          options: {\\n            presets: ['@babel/preset-env', '@babel/preset-react'],\\n          },\\n        },\\n      },\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.css$/,\\n        use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'],\\n      },\\n    ],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    new HtmlWebpackPlugin({\\n      template: './public/index.html',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  resolve: {\\n    extensions: ['.js', '.jsx'],\\n  },\\n};"],[3145,"Tools such as Webpack Bundle Analyzer, which visualise what the build consists of, are used to analyse the bundle size. The size can be reduced through tree shaking (removing unused code), code splitting (breaking the code into chunks), minification (compressing the code) and optimising dependencies (replacing heavy libraries with light equivalents). Lazy loading of modules and removing duplicated dependencies help too.","Analysing the bundle size\\nTo analyse the bundle size in Webpack, the \`webpack-bundle-analyzer\` plugin is used, which creates an interactive map showing which modules take up the most space. That helps identify suboptimal dependencies or duplicated code.\\nMethods of reducing the size\\n\\n- Tree shaking: it removes unused code during the build. For that, use ES modules (\`import/export\`) and set \`sideEffects: false\` in \`package.json\`.\\n- Code splitting: it splits the code into separate chunks loaded on demand. It is implemented through dynamic imports: \`import('./module').then(...)\`.\\n- Minification: it compresses the code by removing whitespace and comments and renaming variables. In Webpack \`TerserPlugin\` is used.\\n- Optimising dependencies: replace heavy libraries (moment.js, for example) with light equivalents (date-fns) or use only the parts you need.\\nAn example Webpack configuration\\n\\nIt is also useful to configure \`externals\` in order to move libraries (React, for example) to a CDN if they do not change.\\nConclusion: analysing and reducing the bundle size is critical for speeding up how the application loads, especially on slow connections. Apply these methods in production builds in order to optimise the user experience.","// webpack.config.js\\nconst BundleAnalyzerPlugin = require('webpack-bundle-analyzer').BundleAnalyzerPlugin;\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  mode: 'production',\\n  optimization: {\\n    usedExports: true, // tree shaking\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      chunks: 'all', // code splitting\\n    },\\n    minimize: true,\\n    minimizer: [new TerserPlugin()],\\n  },\\n  plugins: [\\n    new BundleAnalyzerPlugin(),\\n  ],\\n};"],[3146,"Transitive dependencies are dependencies required not by your project directly but by its direct dependencies. For example, if your project uses library A and library A uses library B, then B is a transitive dependency. Package managers such as npm or Maven resolve and include such dependencies automatically. That makes development easier, but it can lead to version conflicts or a bloated project.","What are transitive dependencies?\\nTransitive dependencies are libraries or packages that are not listed in your project directly but are required for its direct dependencies to work. Imagine you are building a house and you buy a ready-made door (a direct dependency). To install that door you need hinges and a handle — those are the transitive dependencies. You did not order them separately, but they are necessary for the door to work.\\nHow does it work in practice?\\nPackage managers (npm, Maven, pip) build the dependency tree automatically. When you add package A, the system checks its own dependencies (B and C, for example) and includes them recursively. That spares the developer from hunting for every auxiliary library by hand.\\nAn example with npm\\n\\nProblems and management\\n\\n- Version conflicts: two different direct dependencies may require different versions of the same transitive library. Package managers try to resolve that by picking a compatible version or duplicating the package.\\n- Project size: a large number of transitive dependencies can greatly increase the size of node_modules or of the resulting artefact.\\n- Vulnerabilities: transitive dependencies may contain vulnerabilities that you do not control directly. Tools such as npm audit help keep track of them.\\nConclusion\\nTransitive dependencies are an inseparable part of modern development, automating the management of libraries. It is important, though, to keep an eye on their versions and their number in order to avoid conflicts and security problems. Use lock files (package-lock.json) and update dependencies regularly.","// your project's package.json\\n{\\n  \\"dependencies\\": {\\n    \\"express\\": \\"^4.18.0\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// express has its own dependencies, for example accepts, array-flatten\\n// They become transitive for your project\\n\\n// After npm install, node_modules will contain:\\n// node_modules/express\\n// node_modules/accepts (transitive)\\n// node_modules/array-flatten (transitive)"],[3147,"A deterministic dependency installation guarantees that the same input (the manifest file) will produce the same set of packages with the same versions every time. This is achieved with lock files (package-lock.json or yarn.lock, for example), which pin the exact versions of every dependency and sub-dependency. The approach prevents unexpected changes in the project when new versions of packages are released, and ensures the environment is identical on every developer's machine and in CI/CD.","What is a deterministic dependency installation?\\nA deterministic dependency installation is a process in which the package manager (npm, Yarn, pip, for example) guarantees that the same set of inputs (the manifest file, package.json for example) will produce an identical set of installed packages with the same versions. That means the result of the installation is predictable and reproducible on any machine.\\nWhy is it needed?\\nWithout determinism, developers can run into a situation where the project works on one machine but breaks on another because of small differences in dependency versions. That is especially critical in teamwork and when deploying to production. A deterministic installation provides:\\n\\n- Reproducible builds: the same environment is produced every time.\\n- Stability: new versions of packages do not break the project unexpectedly.\\n- Security: you can control exactly which versions are used.\\nHow is it implemented?\\nThe main mechanism is lock files. In the JavaScript ecosystem, for example, package-lock.json (npm) or yarn.lock (Yarn) is used. These files hold the exact versions of every dependency and sub-dependency, along with hashes for checking integrity. An example of package-lock.json's contents:\\n\\nDuring installation the package manager first checks whether a lock file is present. If there is one, it uses the versions from it, ignoring the version ranges given in the manifest. If there is no lock file, one is created from the resolved versions.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose package.json lists the dependency \\"lodash\\": \\"^4.17.0\\". Without a lock file, version 4.17.21 might be downloaded today and 4.18.0 tomorrow, once it is released. With a lock file, the version pinned in it is always installed, 4.17.21 for example.\\nConclusion\\nA deterministic dependency installation is a fundamental practice for making development reliable and predictable. It is essential in any project where a stable environment matters, especially in teamwork and CI/CD. Using lock files and committing them to the repository is the industry standard.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"my-project\\",\\n  \\"lockfileVersion\\": 2,\\n  \\"packages\\": {\\n    \\"node_modules/express\\": {\\n      \\"version\\": \\"4.18.2\\",\\n      \\"resolved\\": \\"https://registry.npmjs.org/express/-/express-4.18.2.tgz\\",\\n      \\"integrity\\": \\"sha512-...\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}"],[3148,"Maven has the scopes compile (the default), provided, runtime, test, system and import. The provided scope means the dependency is needed for compilation but will not be included in the resulting artefact, since the runtime environment is expected to provide it (a servlet container, for example). That is useful for libraries that are already on the server, in order to avoid conflicts and reduce the application's size.","Scopes in Maven\\nBesides the standard compile, test and runtime, Maven provides the scopes provided, system and import. Each of them determines at which stage of the build the dependency is available and whether it will be included in the resulting artefact.\\nThe provided scope\\nThe provided scope states that the dependency is required for compilation but must not be packaged into the resulting JAR/WAR, since the runtime environment is expected to provide it (a servlet container such as Tomcat, or the JDK, for example). That lets you avoid duplicating libraries and version conflicts.\\nAn example: when developing a web application in Java, the Servlet API is usually given the provided scope, since the servlet container already contains that library.\\n\\nThe other scopes\\n\\n- system — similar to provided, but the path to the JAR is given explicitly through a system variable. It is rarely used, since it breaks portability.\\n- import — used only in the <dependencyManagement> section, in order to import dependencies from another POM (a BOM, for example).\\nConclusion\\nThe provided scope is applied to libraries that are already present in the runtime environment, in order to reduce the artefact's size and avoid conflicts. It is standard practice for servlets, JSP and other APIs provided by the container.","<dependency>\\n    <groupId>javax.servlet</groupId>\\n    <artifactId>javax.servlet-api</artifactId>\\n    <version>4.0.1</version>\\n    <scope>provided</scope>\\n</dependency>"],[3149,"Cache busting is a technique that makes the browser download a new version of a static file (CSS, JS), ignoring the old cache. It is usually implemented by adding a unique hash of the contents to the file name. That guarantees users will see the current version of the application after a deployment. Without cache busting the browser may show stale styles or scripts.","What cache busting is and why it is needed\\nCache busting is an approach used to force static resources (JavaScript, CSS, images) in the user's browser to be refreshed after a new version of the application is released. Browsers cache files aggressively in order to speed up page loading, but that can leave users seeing an old version of the interface or broken functionality after a deployment.\\nHow it works\\nThe core idea is to change the file name, or add a unique parameter to it, whenever the contents change. The most reliable way is to use a hash of the file's contents (a content hash). For example, the file \`main.js\` becomes \`main.a1b2c3.js\`. When the contents change, the hash becomes different and the browser treats it as a new resource, downloading it again.\\nAn example implementation with Webpack\\n\\nOn every build Webpack computes the hash from the file's contents. If the code has not changed, the hash stays the same and the browser uses the cached version. That is optimal: users do not download the same files again.\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Module bundlers (Webpack, Vite, Parcel) — they add hashes to file names automatically.\\n- CDNs and static servers — for long-term caching with the ability to update quickly.\\n- Any web application where the interface being up to date and load performance matter.\\nConclusion\\nCache busting is an essential practice for production builds. It lets you combine the benefits of caching (fast loading) with the guarantee that users always get the current files. Without it, bugs involving stale scripts or styles after a deployment are common.","// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  output: {\\n    filename: '[name].[contenthash].js',\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n  },\\n};\\n// The result: main.8f3b2c.js, vendor.4a1d5e.js"],[3150,"CSS and JavaScript files are processed with loaders in Webpack. For CSS, css-loader and style-loader are used, which turn the CSS into JavaScript modules and inject the styles into the DOM. For JavaScript, babel-loader is used to transpile modern code into older versions. These loaders are configured in the Webpack configuration.","Processing CSS and JavaScript in Webpack\\nWebpack is a module bundler that treats every file in a project as a module. Special loaders are used to process CSS and JavaScript, transforming the files before they are added to the resulting bundle.\\nProcessing CSS\\nTwo main loaders are used for CSS: \`css-loader\` and \`style-loader\`. \`css-loader\` interprets \`@import\` and \`url()\` in the CSS, turning it into a JavaScript module. \`style-loader\` then injects those styles into the DOM through a \`<style>\` tag. Alternatively, you can use \`MiniCssExtractPlugin\` in order to extract the CSS into separate files.\\n\\nProcessing JavaScript\\nFor JavaScript, \`babel-loader\` is often used, which transpiles modern ES6+ code into ES5 for browser compatibility. Babel uses presets such as \`@babel/preset-env\` in order to determine the transformations that are needed.\\n\\nThese loaders work in sequence: Webpack applies them right to left (bottom to top in the array). For CSS, \`css-loader\` is applied first, then \`style-loader\`. For JavaScript it is \`babel-loader\`.\\nConclusion: using Webpack's loaders lets you automate the processing of CSS and JavaScript, ensuring compatibility and optimising the code for production.","// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.css$/,\\n        use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader']\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.js$/,\\n        exclude: /node_modules/,\\n        use: {\\n          loader: 'babel-loader',\\n          options: {\\n            presets: ['@babel/preset-env']\\n          }\\n        }\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n};"],[3151,"A source map is a file that links generated (minified or transpiled) code to the original source. It lets developers see and debug the original code in the browser's developer tools, even when a compressed version is used in production. A source map holds information about how lines and characters correspond between the source and the resulting file.","What is a source map?\\nA source map is a technology that lets generated JavaScript code (minified, concatenated or transpiled, for example) be mapped back to its original source. That is critically important for debugging in a production environment, where the code is usually compressed and optimised, which makes it unreadable for a human.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you use a bundler (Webpack, for example) or a transpiler (Babel, for example), it can produce an additional file with the .map extension. That file holds a JSON structure describing how every line and character in the generated code corresponds to the original source. By loading such a .map file (through a special comment at the end of the generated file), the browser can show the original code in the developer tools rather than the compressed one.\\nAn example Webpack configuration\\n\\nIn this example the \`devtool: 'source-map'\` option tells Webpack to create a separate \`bundle.js.map\` file. In production builds, lighter variants are often used, \`'hidden-source-map'\` for example (so as not to expose the source to users), or source maps are disabled entirely.\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\n- Debugging JavaScript/TypeScript in the browser.\\n- Debugging CSS generated from preprocessors (Sass, Less).\\n- Debugging code written in TypeScript, CoffeeScript or other languages compiled to JavaScript.\\nConclusion\\nA source map is an indispensable tool for comfortable debugging of a modern front end. Always use it in development builds, and use it with care in production so as not to expose the source, or use hidden source maps for internal error monitoring.","// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  mode: 'development',\\n  entry: './src/index.js',\\n  output: {\\n    filename: 'bundle.js',\\n    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),\\n  },\\n  devtool: 'source-map', // Enables source map generation\\n};"],[3152,"Optimising the build includes tree shaking to remove unused code, code splitting to break the bundle into parts, minification (TerserPlugin, for example) and compression (gzip/Brotli). Dynamic imports, bundle analysis (webpack-bundle-analyzer) and replacing large libraries with lighter equivalents are used too.","The main approaches to optimising a build\\nOptimising the bundle aims at reducing the page's load time and improving the user experience. The main methods include tree shaking, code splitting, minification and compression.\\nTree shaking\\nTree shaking removes unused code from modules. Static analysis of the imports is used for that. In Webpack it works with ES modules (import/export). Example:\\n\\nCode splitting\\nCode splitting breaks the bundle into parts that are loaded on demand. That reduces the size of the initial download. A dynamic import is used:\\n\\nMinification and compression\\nMinification removes whitespace and comments and shortens variable names. Webpack uses TerserPlugin. Compression (gzip/Brotli) on the server reduces the amount of data transferred.\\nAdditional methods\\n\\n- Analysing the bundle with webpack-bundle-analyzer in order to identify large dependencies.\\n- Replacing large libraries (moment.js with date-fns, for example).\\n- Using a CDN for static resources.\\n- Optimising images (compression, lazy loading).\\nConclusion: apply tree shaking and code splitting in modern applications and minification for production, and bundle analysis will help you identify the bottlenecks. This is especially useful for large projects with many dependencies.","// utils.js\\nexport function used() { return 1; }\\nexport function unused() { return 2; }\\n// main.js\\nimport { used } from './utils';\\nconsole.log(used()); // unused() will be removed\\n\\n// React lazy loading\\nconst LazyComponent = React.lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'));\\n// Webpack dynamic import\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\\n  import('./module').then(module => module.default());\\n});"],[3153,"Code splitting lets you break one large application into several parts that are loaded as they are needed. In React it is done with dynamic imports and React.lazy. The main principle is to split by route or by large component, so that the user gets only the code they need. That speeds up the initial load and saves traffic.","What code splitting is and why it is needed\\nWhen a React application is bundled into a single chunk, all the code — libraries, components, utilities — ends up in one file. On a first visit to the site the browser downloads that whole file, even if the user only sees the login page. That slows loading down and increases data usage. Code splitting solves the problem: the application is broken into several parts that are loaded only when they are really needed.\\nHow it works in React\\nThe main tool is the dynamic import. Instead of the static \`import Component from './Component'\`, you use \`const Component = React.lazy(() => import('./Component'))\`. React.lazy lets a component's loading be deferred until the moment it is rendered. The \`Suspense\` component is used to handle the loading state, showing a placeholder while the chunk loads.\\n\\nThe principles of splitting\\nCode should be split along the application's logical boundaries. The most common approach is splitting by route: each page becomes a separate chunk. You can also move heavy libraries (charts or editors, for example) into separate chunks, so that they do not slow the initial load down. It is important not to fragment the code too finely — each chunk carries the overhead of an HTTP request, so it is optimal for a chunk to be no smaller than 10–20 KB.\\nConfiguring it in Webpack\\nWebpack creates separate chunks for dynamic imports automatically. In addition, you can configure \`splitChunks\` in order to move shared dependencies (React, for example) into a separate vendor chunk. That lets the browser cache unchanging libraries separately from the application's code.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCode splitting is standard practice for large React applications. It speeds up the first load, reduces the load on the network and improves the user experience. Apply it whenever an application has several routes or heavy components that are not needed at start-up.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));\\nconst Settings = lazy(() => import('./Settings'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading…</div>}>\\n      <Dashboard />\\n      <Settings />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      chunks: 'all',\\n      cacheGroups: {\\n        vendor: {\\n          test: /[\\\\\\\\/]node_modules[\\\\\\\\/]/,\\n          name: 'vendors',\\n          chunks: 'all',\\n        },\\n      },\\n    },\\n  },\\n};"],[3154,"Usually not — if a library is not imported anywhere in the code, it does not end up in the production build. Modern bundlers (Webpack, Rollup, Vite) analyse the dependency graph and exclude unused modules. This only works for ES modules (import/export), though, not for CommonJS (require). It also matters that the library is written with tree shaking support.","How bundlers handle unused libraries\\nWhen you build a project for production, the bundler (Webpack, for example) builds the dependency graph starting from the entry point. If a library is not imported anywhere, it does not enter that graph and therefore is not included in the resulting bundle. This is called dead code elimination and is part of the tree shaking process.\\nThe conditions for effective tree shaking\\n\\n- Using ES modules (import/export) — they can be analysed statically, unlike CommonJS (require), which is dynamic.\\n- The library must be written with tree shaking support (often declared in package.json through the \`sideEffects: false\` field).\\n- The bundler must be set to production mode (\`mode: 'production'\` in Webpack, for example), which enables minification and the removal of unused code.\\nExample\\n\\nIf you wrote \`import _ from 'lodash'\` but did not use \`_\`, then depending on the settings the bundler might remove the import, but if the library uses CommonJS it may end up in the bundle whole.\\nExceptions and subtleties\\nSome libraries have side effects on import (polyfills, for example), so they cannot simply be removed. For such cases, package.json lists \`sideEffects\` as an array of the files that must be kept. Also, if a library is imported in some module but that module itself is not used, the library can be excluded only if the whole module is removed.\\nConclusion\\nIn most cases an unused library will not end up in the production build, provided you use a modern bundler and ES modules. That helps reduce the bundle size and speed up how the application loads. For reliability, though, check the bundler's settings and make sure the libraries are compatible with tree shaking.","// main.js\\nimport { usedFunction } from './utils';\\n// The lodash library is not imported, so it will not end up in the bundle\\nconsole.log(usedFunction());\\n\\n// utils.js\\nexport function usedFunction() { return 'ok'; }\\nexport function unusedFunction() { return 'no'; } // it will be removed by tree shaking"],[3155,"Modern bundlers such as Vite and Rollup differ from Webpack in speed and simplicity. Vite uses native ES modules in dev mode and esbuild for fast building, while Rollup is optimised for libraries. Webpack is more powerful and flexible but more complex and slower. Moving to Vite is worth it for new projects and fast development, to Rollup for publishing libraries, while Webpack is best left for complex legacy projects with unique plugins.","An introduction to the problem of choosing a bundler\\nWebpack was the de facto standard for building front-end applications for a long time. Its configuration is often unwieldy, though, and its speed leaves something to be desired, especially on large projects. Modern tools such as Vite and Rollup offer a different approach, based on using the browser's native capabilities and more efficient algorithms. Understanding their differences helps a developer choose a tool deliberately for the task at hand rather than out of habit.\\nThe key differences between Vite, Rollup and Webpack\\nVite's main difference is that in dev mode it does not build the whole project up front. Instead it uses the browser's native ES modules, serving the source code on request. That makes starting the server practically instant and hot module replacement very fast. For the production build Vite uses Rollup, which optimises the code excellently. Rollup, for its part, specialises in tree shaking and producing clean, compact bundles, which is ideal for libraries. Webpack, by contrast, always performs a full static build, which gives it enormous flexibility through plugins and loaders, but at the cost of complexity and speed.\\nA practical example: a Vite configuration\\n\\nThis config replaces dozens of lines of Webpack setup. By comparison, in Webpack you would need to configure babel-loader, resolve.extensions, devServer and much more. Vite takes most of the routine tasks on itself, letting the developer concentrate on the code.\\nWhen it is worth moving to a modern bundler\\nMoving to Vite is justified for new projects where development speed and simple configuration matter. It suits SPAs, SSR and even static sites well. Rollup is the best choice for publishing npm libraries, since it generates clean ESM and CJS bundles. Webpack remains relevant for complex enterprise projects that already have many custom plugins, or when every aspect of the build needs fine tuning, for micro-frontends for example.\\nConclusion\\nChoosing a bundler is a trade-off between speed, simplicity and flexibility. For most new projects Vite will be an excellent start, and Rollup a reliable tool for libraries. Webpack is not obsolete, but it should be applied deliberately, when its power is genuinely necessary. Understanding these subtleties lets you make well-considered architectural decisions.","// vite.config.js\\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite';\\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';\\n\\nexport default defineConfig({\\n  plugins: [react()],\\n  server: {\\n    port: 3000,\\n  },\\n  build: {\\n    outDir: 'dist',\\n    sourcemap: true,\\n  },\\n});"],[3156,"Micro-frontends can be included in two main ways: at build time and at runtime. At build time the host imports the remote module as an ordinary dependency and it ends up in the bundle. At runtime the host loads the remote code dynamically through Module Federation, script tags or an iframe. The choice depends on the requirements for deployment independence and how coupled the teams are.","Micro-frontends are an architectural approach in which a large front-end application is broken into several independently developed and deployed parts. The key question is how the host application (the shell) learns about the micro-frontends and includes them in its build or loads them while running.\\nThe main ways of including them\\n\\n- Build-time integration — the micro-frontend is published as an npm package and the host imports it as an ordinary module. Everything ends up in one bundle.\\n- Runtime integration through Module Federation — the host and the remote modules are built separately and exchange modules at runtime through a shared container.\\n- Dynamic script loading — the host loads the micro-frontend's JS bundle through a script tag and mounts it into the DOM.\\n- An iframe — the micro-frontend is isolated in a separate document, communicating through postMessage.\\nAn example with Module Federation\\nIn the Webpack configuration the host declares the remote modules, and the micro-frontend declares what it exports.\\n\\nHow it ends up in the build\\nAt build time everything is included in one bundle at compile time. At runtime the host contains none of the micro-frontend's code — it merely references remoteEntry.js, which is loaded in the browser. That lets micro-frontends be deployed independently, without rebuilding the host.\\nIn conclusion: build time is simpler but couples releases together; runtime through Module Federation gives deployment independence and suits large teams, while iframes and script tags are used for isolation or for legacy systems.","// the host's webpack.config.js\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  remotes: {\\n    products: 'products@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js'\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// the micro-frontend's webpack.config.js\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: 'products',\\n  filename: 'remoteEntry.js',\\n  exposes: {\\n    './ProductList': './src/ProductList'\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// Using it in the host\\nconst ProductList = React.lazy(() => import('products/ProductList'));"],[3157,"Micro-frontends are needed when many teams work on one large application and get in each other's way at release time. Splitting it up lets each team deploy its own part independently. The criteria are: the size of the team, the frequency of releases, different technologies, isolation of domains. If the application is small, though, micro-frontends will add unnecessary complexity.","Micro-frontends are an architectural approach in which a single front-end application is broken into several independently developed and deployed parts. Each part is responsible for its own business domain and can use its own stack, have its own release cycle and its own team.\\nReasons for moving to them\\n\\n- A growing team: when 20+ developers work on one SPA, releases become the bottleneck.\\n- Independent deployment: one team can ship a feature without waiting for the others.\\n- Different technologies: an old Angular module and a new React module can coexist.\\n- Fault isolation: one micro-frontend failing does not necessarily break the whole application.\\nThe criteria for splitting\\n\\n- There are clear business domains (catalogue, basket, profile).\\n- The teams are autonomous and own their domains end to end.\\n- An independent release cycle is required.\\n- The application is already large and a monolithic front end is slowing development down.\\nAn example with Module Federation\\n\\nHere the host application loads the remote catalogue module at runtime, and the shared dependencies (React) are shared so that they are not duplicated.\\nWhen not to do it\\nIf the application is small, there is one team and releases do not conflict, micro-frontends will bring more problems than benefits: the build becomes more complex, the bundle size grows, and problems with shared state and styles appear.\\nIn conclusion: micro-frontends are worth applying when teams are scaling up and independent releases per business domain are needed; in small projects they are excessive.","// host/webpack.config.js\\nnew ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n  name: 'host',\\n  remotes: {\\n    catalog: 'catalog@https://cdn.example.com/remoteEntry.js'\\n  },\\n  shared: ['react', 'react-dom']\\n});\\n\\n// In the host's code\\nconst Catalog = React.lazy(() => import('catalog/App'));"],[3158,"HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure) is the secure version of the HTTP protocol, which uses SSL/TLS for encrypting the data. It works over port 443 and protects the data transferred from being intercepted or altered.","The HTTPS protocol is an extension of the HTTP protocol, which provides a secure connection between the client (a browser, for example) and the server. It uses:\\nSSL/TLS:\\n\\n- It encrypts the data transferred, so that it cannot be intercepted.\\n\\n- It provides the authentication of the server through certificates.\\n\\nPort 443:\\n\\n- That is the standard port for HTTPS.\\n\\nAn example of how HTTPS works:\\n\\n- The user visits a secure site (https://example.com, for example).\\n\\n- The browser requests the certificate from the server.\\n\\n- The server sends the certificate, which confirms its authenticity.\\n\\n- An encrypted connection is established.\\n\\nHTTPS is necessary for protecting data, especially on sites where confidential data is entered (passwords, card numbers).",null],[3159,"The application layer provides such services as email (SMTP), the transfer of files (FTP), the management of access (SSH) and a network virtual terminal (Telnet).","The application layer is the top layer of the network model, which provides the interface between the user and the network. The main services provided:\\n\\n- Mail services:\\nAn example: SMTP for sending letters, POP3/IMAP for receiving them.\\n\\n- File transfer:\\nAn example: FTP for uploading and downloading files.\\n\\n- Access management:\\nAn example: SSH for connecting to remote servers safely.\\n\\n- A network virtual terminal:\\nAn example: Telnet, which lets you manage a remote device.\\n\\n- Directory services:\\nAn example: LDAP for searching for information about users and devices.\\n\\nThe application layer plays the key role in providing the user's interaction with the network through the various applications.",null],[3160,"The headers are added at layers 6 (the presentation layer), 5 (the session layer), 4 (the transport layer) and 3 (the network layer). The trailer is added at the data-link layer (layer 2).","In the OSI model the data is encapsulated at every layer, with headers and sometimes trailers added:\\n\\n- Layer 6 (the presentation layer): it adds a header related to the data's format (the encoding, for example).\\n\\n- Layer 5 (the session layer): it adds a header for managing the communication session (the session's identifiers, for example).\\n\\n- Layer 4 (the transport layer): it adds a header with information about the port numbers and the segmentation.\\n\\n- Layer 3 (the network layer): it adds a header with the sender's and the recipient's IP addresses.\\n\\n- The data-link layer (layer 2): it adds a header for the addressing at the MAC level, plus a trailer for checking the data's integrity (the CRC).\\n\\nThe addition of the headers and the trailers lets every layer process the data properly and pass it between the nodes.",null],[3161,"When a packet of data moves from the lower to the upper layer of the OSI model, the headers added earlier are removed, and only the original data is left.","The process of decapsulation includes:\\n\\n- The data-link layer (layer 2): it removes the trailer and the header, leaving the frame.\\n\\n- The network layer (layer 3): it removes the network header, leaving the segment or the message.\\n\\n- The transport layer (layer 4): it takes the information about the ports away, leaving the data for the session layer.\\n\\n- The presentation and the session layers (layers 5–6): they take the service data away, leaving the original content, ready to be passed to the application.\\n\\nThe removal of the headers lets the original data sent by the sender be restored.",null],[3162,"When a packet of data moves from the upper to the lower layer of the OSI model, headers are added at every layer. Those headers contain the information needed for transferring the data between the devices.","The process of encapsulation includes:\\n\\n- The presentation and the session layers (layers 6–5): they add headers for the encoding and the management of the session.\\n\\n- The transport layer (layer 4): it adds the information about the segmentation of the data and the port numbers (TCP/UDP).\\n\\n- The network layer (layer 3): it adds the IP header with the sender's and the recipient's addresses.\\n\\n- The data-link layer (layer 2): it adds the MAC addresses plus a trailer for checking for errors.\\n\\n- The physical layer (layer 1): it transforms the data into electrical signals or radio waves for the transmission.\\n\\nThe encapsulation of the data lets every layer of the OSI model prepare the information for a successful transmission over the network.",null],[3163,"A zone-based firewall is an improved method of a stateful firewall. It organizes the traffic between trusted and untrusted zones on the basis of security rules applied to the traffic between the zones.","The principles on which a zone-based firewall works:\\n1. The definition of the zones: every interface of the device (a router, for example) is assigned a particular zone, such as the \\"internal network\\" (trusted) or the \\"external network\\" (untrusted).\\n2. The application of the rules: the traffic between the zones is permitted or blocked on the basis of predefined security policies. For example:\\n\\n- Forbid access from the \\"external network\\" to the \\"internal network\\".\\n\\n- Permit access to particular services, such as a web server or a mail server.\\n\\n3. The preservation of the state: a zone-based firewall preserves the information about the current connections in a stateful table, which lets it pass only the responses permitted.\\nThe ways of implementing it:\\n\\n- CBAC (Context-Based Access Control): the traditional method of creating ACLs (access control lists) for managing the traffic.\\n\\n- A Zone-Based Firewall: the more modern approach, based on the management of the traffic between the zones.\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Improved security, thanks to the clear separation of the zones.\\n\\n- Convenience in managing the security rules.\\n\\nZone-based firewalls suit the management of complex networks, where a clear separation of the trusted and the untrusted segments is required.",null],[3164,"A server farm is a group of servers united for performing tasks with high performance. They are used for hosting websites, for cloud services and for complex computation.","The main characteristics of a server farm:\\n\\n- The uniting of the servers: the servers are united into a single infrastructure for working together.\\n\\n- The distribution of the load: load balancing is used for distributing the requests between the servers evenly.\\n\\n- Improved performance: it lets you handle a great many requests at once, increasing the speed and the efficiency.\\n\\n- The application:\\n\\n- The hosting of websites.\\n\\n- The processing of big data.\\n\\n- The performing of complex computation — in scientific research, for example.\\n\\nAn example of how it works:\\nA server farm used for hosting distributes the users' requests through a load balancer, which redirects the traffic to the servers available.\\nServer farms provide high performance and fault tolerance, which makes them a key component of modern data centres.",null],[3165,"The three methods of authentication are biometrics (a fingerprint, for example), a token (an electronic device or a card) and a password. To improve the security you can use two-factor authentication, combining two of those methods.","The main methods of authentication:\\n1. Passwords:\\n\\n- A simple, widespread means of protection.\\n\\n- It is vulnerable to guessing or cracking, if the passwords are weak.\\n\\n2. Biometrics:\\n\\n- The use of a person's unique characteristics, such as fingerprints, an iris scan or facial recognition.\\n\\n- A high level of protection, but it requires special equipment.\\n\\n3. Tokens:\\n\\n- Physical devices, such as smart cards or USB keys, which generate one-off codes.\\n\\n- They are reliable, but they can be lost.\\n\\nTwo-factor authentication (2FA):\\nThe combination of two methods (a password and a token, for example) improves the security considerably, reducing the probability of unauthorized access.\\nAn example: to log in to a bank account, the user types a password (the 1st factor) and confirms the login through an SMS code (the 2nd factor).\\nThe use of several levels of authentication helps you protect the systems and the data from unauthorized access.",null],[3166,"The CIA triad is the foundation of information security:\\n\\n- Confidentiality: the data is protected from unauthorized access.\\n\\n- Integrity: the data is accurate and has not been altered without permission.\\n\\n- Availability: the data and the systems are available when they are needed.","1. Confidentiality:\\n\\n- It provides the protection of the data from unauthorized access.\\n\\n- The methods: encryption, access control, passwords.\\n\\n- An example: your password should not be known to other people.\\n\\n2. Integrity:\\n\\n- It guarantees that the data stays accurate and complete.\\n\\n- Only authorized users can make changes.\\n\\n- An example: after an employee leaves, the data about them is updated only by particular specialists.\\n\\n3. Availability:\\n\\n- The data and the systems have to be available to the authorized users at the time needed.\\n\\n- The threats: DoS attacks, failures of the equipment.\\n\\n- An example: the system has to be working, so that the employees can check the information about their tasks.\\n\\nThe CIA triad helps you develop reliable, secure systems, minimizing the risks to the data.",null],[3167,"A VPN (Virtual Private Network) creates a secure connection over public networks, such as the internet. It lets users connect to a private network remotely, providing the encryption of the data.","1. What a VPN is:\\n\\n- It is a technology for creating a virtual secure channel of communication over an insecure network (the internet, for example).\\n\\n- The data is encrypted, which prevents the information being intercepted by attackers.\\n\\n2. The application:\\n\\n- The protection of confidential information in publicly accessible networks.\\n\\n- Remote work: employees can connect to the corporate network while they are elsewhere.\\n\\n- Getting round geographical restrictions (access to blocked sites, for example).\\n\\n3. How it works:\\n\\n- It uses tunnelling protocols for protecting the data.\\n\\n- Examples of the protocols: OpenVPN, PPTP, L2TP.\\n\\nA VPN is widely used for protecting data and for providing remote access, especially in the corporate environment.",null],[3168,"Symmetric encryption: one key is used for the encryption and the decryption. That is fast, but it requires the key to be transferred safely.\\nAsymmetric encryption: it uses a pair of keys (a public one and a private one). That is safer, but slower.","1. Symmetric encryption:\\n\\n- One and the same key is used for the encryption and the decryption.\\n\\n- The advantages: a high speed of work.\\n\\n- The disadvantages: the key has to be transferred safely.\\n\\n- An example: the AES and DES algorithms.\\n\\n2. Asymmetric encryption:\\n\\n- Two keys are used: a public one (for the encryption) and a private one (for the decryption).\\n\\n- The advantages: the safe transfer of the data.\\n\\n- The disadvantages: a low speed.\\n\\n- An example: RSA.\\n\\nConclusion: symmetric encryption suits large volumes of data, while asymmetric encryption suits the safe transfer of the keys.","from cryptography.fernet import Fernet\\nkey = Fernet.generate_key()\\ncipher = Fernet(key)\\nencrypted = cipher.encrypt(b\\"Confidential Data\\")\\ndecrypted = cipher.decrypt(encrypted)\\n\\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import rsa\\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import padding\\nprivate_key = rsa.generate_private_key(public_exponent=65537, key_size=2048)\\npublic_key = private_key.public_key()\\nencrypted = public_key.encrypt(b\\"Confidential Data\\", padding.OAEP(\\n      padding.MGF1(algorithm=hashes.SHA256()), hashes.SHA256(), None))"],[3169,"IPsec works at layer 3 of the OSI model (the network layer). It provides the protection of the data at the level of the IP packets, using methods of encryption and authentication.","1. What is IPsec?\\n\\n- IPsec (Internet Protocol Security) is a set of protocols for ensuring the safe transfer of data over IP networks.\\n\\n- It is used for the encryption, for checking the data's integrity and for the authentication of the source.\\n\\n2. At which layer it works:\\nIPsec functions at the network layer (Layer 3) of the OSI model. That lets it protect the IP packets and interact with different networks.\\n3. The advantages:\\n\\n- It provides security for every application that uses IP.\\n\\n- It suits the creation of VPN connections.\\n\\nIPsec is a security standard at the network layer, which is widely used for ensuring a secure interaction between networks.",null],[3170,"Tunnel mode is a way of transferring data in which the devices do not use IPsec directly. Instead a virtual tunnel is created through gateways, which provides the encryption of all of the traffic between the nodes.","1. What is tunnel mode?\\n\\n- Tunnel mode is used for protecting data between two networks over a shared channel.\\n\\n- All of the data, including the original IP headers, is encrypted and placed in new IP packets.\\n\\n2. The application:\\n\\n- It is used for encrypting the traffic between secure gateways.\\n\\n- An example: the connection between a corporate Cisco router and a PIX Firewall.\\n\\n3. The advantages:\\n\\n- It ensures the security of the data when it is transferred over insecure networks.\\n\\n- It is convenient for organizing a VPN between several networks.\\n\\nTunnel mode is useful when data has to be protected between two points, ensuring it is encrypted completely.",null],[3171,"A digital signature is a mathematical method of checking the authenticity and the integrity of electronic messages, documents or software. It is used for preventing the forgery of data and for confirming its source.","1. What is a digital signature?\\n\\n- It is the electronic equivalent of a handwritten signature, which ensures the authenticity of the data.\\n\\n- It works on the basis of cryptography with a public and a private key.\\n\\n2. How it works:\\n\\n- The sender uses the private key for creating the signature (the hashing of the data).\\n\\n- The recipient uses the public key for checking the signature.\\n\\n- If the data has been altered, the signature becomes invalid.\\n\\n3. The application:\\n\\n- The confirmation of the sender (the authentication).\\n\\n- The protection of the data from alteration (the integrity).\\n\\n- An example: signed electronic documents or secure transactions.\\n\\nAn example of the code (Python):\\n\\nDigital signatures are a reliable way of checking the authenticity of data and protecting it in the digital world.","from cryptography.hazmat.primitives import hashes\\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import padding, rsa\\n\\n# The generation of the keys\\nprivate_key = rsa.generate_private_key(public_exponent=65537, key_size=2048)\\npublic_key = private_key.public_key()\\n\\n# The creation of the signature\\nmessage = b\\"Secure Message\\"\\nsignature = private_key.sign(message, padding.PSS(mgf=padding.MGF1(hashes.SHA256()), salt_length=padding.PSS.MAX_LENGTH), hashes.SHA256())\\n\\n# The checking of the signature\\ntry:\\n      public_key.verify(signature, message, padding.PSS(mgf=padding.MGF1(hashes.SHA256()), salt_length=padding.PSS.MAX_LENGTH), hashes.SHA256())\\n      print(\\"The signature is valid\\")\\nexcept Exception:\\n      print(\\"The signature is invalid\\")"],[3172,"Authorization is the process that determines which resources and actions are available to a user once the authentication has succeeded. It is used for applying the security policies, in order to limit the access to the network's resources on the basis of the user's rights.","1. What is authorization?\\n\\n- The authorization happens after the authentication, when the user has already proved their identity.\\n\\n- It decides which resources the user can use and which operations they are permitted to perform.\\n\\n2. The application:\\n\\n- In a company that can be the access to files, to databases or to a management system.\\n\\n- Only an administrator can change the system's settings, for example, while an ordinary user can only view the data.\\n\\n3. The process:\\n\\n- Once the authentication has succeeded, the system checks what access rights the user has on the basis of their role (an administrator, a manager, an employee, for example).\\n\\n- An example: a user who has been authenticated through a VPN can have access to particular servers or applications, depending on their role.\\n\\nAuthorization is necessary for implementing a security policy and for the separation of the access to the network's resources on the basis of the users' roles.",null],[3173,"An IPS (Intrusion Prevention System) is a system for detecting and preventing attacks, which analyses the traffic for malicious activity and takes steps to block it. A firewall, on the other hand, filters the traffic on the basis of predefined rules, controlling which connections are permitted and which are blocked.","1. An IPS's functions:\\n\\n- An IPS analyses the network traffic, reveals malicious actions and tries to prevent them.\\n\\n- It can block an attack in real time, or change the network's security in order to prevent its success.\\n\\n2. A firewall's functions:\\n\\n- A firewall is a device that filters the traffic, permitting or forbidding it depending on the rules configured.\\n\\n- It can block the access by IP address, by port or by protocol.\\n\\n3. The main differences:\\n\\n- An IPS responds to the threats actively and tries to block them, whereas a firewall only filters the traffic by the rules established in advance.\\n\\n- A firewall can be part of an IPS, but an IPS also uses more complex methods for preventing attacks, such as the analysis of the traffic's behaviour.\\n\\nAn example:\\nIf an IPS detects an attempt at an SQL injection in a request, it can block it, whereas a firewall can simply not let the traffic from an unknown IP address through.\\nAn IPS is a more active security system, aimed at preventing attacks in real time, whereas a firewall performs the simpler task of blocking undesirable connections.",null],[3174,"IP spoofing is a technique in which an attacker substitutes their IP address, in order to hide the traffic's true source. That is used for masking attacks such as a DDoS, or for getting round security measures.","1. What is IP spoofing?\\n\\n- IP spoofing lets an attacker substitute the IP address, so that the traffic seems to come from another device.\\n\\n- That technique is used for hiding an attack's real source, or for getting round the filtering at the level of the network.\\n\\n2. How does it work?\\n\\n- The attacker sends packets with forged IP headers, which makes it impossible to track the traffic's real source.\\n\\n- That technique is often used when carrying out Denial of Service (DoS) attacks, when the attacker wants to hide their real IP address.\\n\\n3. The application in an attack:\\n\\n- In a DDoS attack, for example, the attacker can send requests with forged IP addresses, making the attack's real initiator hard to detect.\\n\\nIP spoofing is a dangerous technique, which uses the substitution of IP addresses for hiding the traffic's source and for carrying out attacks. Protection from such attacks requires careful configuration of the filtering and the monitoring of the network traffic.",null],[3175,"A threat is something that can use a vulnerability in order to destroy or damage an asset (data, for example).\\nA vulnerability is a weak point in the protection, which can be used by a threat.\\nA risk is the combination of an asset, a threat and a vulnerability. It is the probability that a threat will use a vulnerability in order to do damage to the asset.","1. A threat:\\n\\n- A threat can be either deliberate (a hacker attack, for example) or accidental (the loss of data because of a failure of the equipment, for example).\\n\\n- An example: a hacker using a vulnerability in a system in order to steal confidential information.\\n\\n2. A vulnerability:\\n\\n- A vulnerability is a weakness or a mistake in the protection, which can be used by a threat in order to do damage.\\n\\n- An example: outdated software, which has known vulnerabilities.\\n\\n3. A risk:\\n\\n- A risk is the probability that a threat will use a vulnerability in order to do damage to an asset. It also includes the possible consequences for the asset.\\n\\n- The formula:\\nThreat (T) + Vulnerability (V) = Risk (R)\\n\\n- An example: if you have confidential information (the asset) and your system has a vulnerability (weak passwords, for example), then the risk will be high if a threat (a hacker) uses that vulnerability for access to the information.\\n\\nAn understanding of the threats, the vulnerabilities and the risks helps organizations manage their security effectively and minimize the potential losses.",null],[3176,"DNS (the Domain Name System) is a system that translates domain names into the IP addresses needed for connecting to resources on the internet. That lets the user use easily remembered addresses instead of numeric IP addresses.","1. DNS's main role:\\n\\n- DNS helps devices find the resources they need on the internet, by transforming domain names into IP addresses.\\n\\n- If you type the address \\"example.com\\" in the browser, for example, the DNS server transforms it into an IP address — 192.0.2.1, for example — so that the device can establish a connection with the server.\\n\\n2. How DNS works:\\n\\n- When you type an address in the browser, the request is sent to a DNS server, which looks for the corresponding IP address.\\n\\n- If the server has no record, it requests the information from other DNS servers until it obtains the IP needed.\\n\\n3. The role of the DNS servers:\\n\\n- On the internet there are a great many DNS servers, which contain the records about the domain names and their IP addresses.\\n\\n- The DNS system is distributed and works on the principle of a hierarchy: from the root servers to the servers responsible for particular domains.\\n\\nDNS servers are an important part of the internet's infrastructure, providing the ability to use convenient domain names instead of numeric addresses for communicating with the resources on the network.",null],[3177,"DNS uses the TCP and UDP protocols. The port for DNS requests is 53.","1. The protocols for DNS:\\n\\n- DNS uses both TCP and UDP.\\n\\n- UDP is used for most DNS requests, since that protocol is faster and more efficient for small packets of data.\\n\\n- TCP is used for transferring larger data — when transferring zone files, for example.\\n\\n2. DNS's port:\\n\\n- The standard port for DNS requests is 53.\\n\\n- That port is used for both TCP and UDP connections.\\n\\n3. The application:\\n\\n- When you request a domain — \\"www.example.com\\", for example — the request is passed to the DNS server through port 53 over the UDP protocol, if the response is small.\\n\\n- In the case of more complex requests or of large responses, TCP can be used.\\n\\nAn understanding of DNS's protocols and ports matters for the configuration and the troubleshooting of a network, and for ensuring the security of the DNS servers.",null],[3178,"The transmission media (cables, for example) work at the physical layer (Layer 1) of the OSI model. That layer is responsible for the transmission of raw data over physical channels — through cables or radio waves, for example.","1. The physical layer:\\n\\n- The physical layer (Layer 1) of the OSI model is responsible for the physical transmission of the data over the network.\\n\\n- It determines the electrical, mechanical and procedural characteristics for the activation, the transmission and the reception of raw bits over a physical channel.\\n\\n- At that layer sit such components as cables, connectors, radio waves and optical fibre.\\n\\n2. The role of the transmission media:\\n\\n- The transmission media are the physical infrastructure over which the data is transferred.\\n\\n- They can be twisted pairs, fibre-optic cables or radio waves, for example. Those media provide the \\"carrying\\" of the data, but they do not process the data.\\n\\n3. An example of the application:\\n\\n- When you transfer data over a network — through Ethernet, for example — the physical layer is responsible for transforming the data into signals that can be sent over the cable or over the air.\\n\\nAn understanding that the transmission media belong to the physical layer helps with the proper configuration and maintenance of a network, and with the diagnosis of problems with the connection.",null],[3179,"A checksum cannot detect multi-bit errors, when several bits of the data change at once, especially if they compensate for one another.","1. A checksum:\\n\\n- A checksum is a method of checking the integrity of data, which is used for detecting errors during the transmission or the storage of the data.\\n\\n- It is computed by adding all of the bits or bytes in the message up, after which the remainder of the division by a given number is computed (normally by 255 or 65535).\\n\\n2. Multi-bit errors:\\n\\n- Multi-bit errors, when several bits of the data change at once, cannot always be detected by means of a checksum.\\n\\n- An example: if two bits have changed and their changes compensate for one another (one bit has changed to 1 and the other to 0, for example), then the checksum will not detect that error.\\n\\n3. The application:\\nChecksums are normally used in protocols such as UDP and TCP, but they do not guarantee the complete detection of errors, especially for more complex errors. For a more reliable check of the data's integrity, more complex methods are often used, such as cyclic redundancy codes (the CRC).\\nAlthough a checksum is effective for detecting some types of error, it is not ideal for detecting every kind of error, and in the case of critical applications more complex methods of checking should be used.",null],[3180,"A reverse proxy server takes the requests from the clients and redirects them to the corresponding web servers, hiding them from the end user. It can also be used for protecting confidential data on the servers.","1. A reverse proxy's functions:\\nThe redirection of the requests: a reverse proxy takes the requests from the clients (web browsers, for example) and redirects them to the internal web servers. That lets you hide the information about the internal servers and their architecture from the clients.\\nLoad balancing: a reverse proxy can also serve for load balancing, by distributing the requests between several web servers, in order to prevent individual servers being overloaded.\\nCaching: reverse proxies can cache frequently requested resources, in order to speed the handling of the requests up and reduce the load on the servers.\\n2. The use in security:\\nThe protection of confidential data: a reverse proxy limits the direct access to the internal servers and protects them from possible threats. It can filter the requests and block potentially dangerous traffic, improving the level of security.\\nSSL termination: a reverse proxy can be used for terminating the SSL connections, decrypting the traffic before passing it to the web servers, which speeds the handling of the requests up.",null],[3181,"To obtain a domain name's IP address, you can use the ping or nslookup command in the command line or the terminal.","1. The use of the PING command:\\n\\n- The ping command sends packets to the domain and obtains its IP address in the response. An example:\\n\\n- That command lets you find out which IP address corresponds to the domain name on the network.\\n\\n2. The use of the nslookup command:\\n\\n- The nslookup command provides more detailed information about the domain's DNS records, including its IP addresses. An example:\\n\\n- That lets you obtain information about the domains and their related IP addresses, plus other DNS records, such as the MX records (for the mail).\\n\\nTo obtain a domain's IP address you can use the ping or nslookup commands, which perform DNS requests and print the corresponding IP address.","ping example.com\\n\\nnslookup example.com"],[3182,"In TCP flow control is carried out by means of the sliding-window protocol, in which the receiving side states the window's size, which shows the number of bytes it can accept.","1. The sliding-window protocol:\\n\\n- In TCP flow control is carried out by means of the sliding-window protocol. That lets it control the volume of data the sender can transfer before receiving an acknowledgement from the recipient.\\n\\n- The receiver announces the window's size, which shows how many bytes it can accept without overloading its buffer. The sender cannot transfer more data than is stated in the window.\\n\\n2. The role of flow control in TCP:\\n\\n- This mechanism prevents the receiving device being overloaded, reducing the probability of the loss of data.\\n\\n- The receiver can adjust the window's size depending on its ability to accept data.\\n\\n3. An example:\\nIf the window is 10 KB in size, the sender can send no more than 10 KB of data before it has to receive an acknowledgement from the receiver. When the receiver is ready to accept more data, it can increase the window's size.\\nTCP uses the sliding-window mechanism for managing the flow of the data efficiently, which lets it maintain a balance between the performance and the reliability of the transmission.",null],[3183,"FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is a network protocol used for transferring files between devices over the internet, using the client-server model. Despite the fact that it is being replaced by safer protocols such as SFTP and SCP, FTP is still widely used.","1. FTP (File Transfer Protocol):\\n\\n- FTP lets users transfer files between a client and a server over a network. It is one of the oldest protocols for exchanging files and works on the client-server model.\\n\\n- The FTP protocol can work in both active and passive mode. In active mode the client initiates the connection with the server, while in passive mode the server listens on a particular port and waits for a connection from the client.\\n\\n2. FTP's limitations:\\n\\n- Despite its wide use, FTP provides no encryption of the data, which makes it vulnerable to attacks. Because of that it is beginning to be replaced by safer protocols, such as SFTP (Secure File Transfer Protocol) and SCP (Secure Copy Protocol).\\n\\n- SFTP and SCP use SSH for protecting the data transferred, providing the security and the confidentiality.\\n\\nFTP is the standard protocol for transferring files, but given its drawbacks in security, it is gradually being replaced by more secure alternatives, such as SFTP and SCP.",null],[3184,"There are four types of IP address: public IP addresses, private IP addresses, static IP addresses and dynamic IP addresses. They differ in their function and their behaviour.","1. Public IP addresses:\\nPublic IP addresses are used for the communication of devices on the internet. Those addresses are unique and are assigned by the internet provider. A public IP address lets devices interact with other devices on the global network.\\n2. Private IP addresses:\\nPrivate IP addresses are used for networks that are not connected to the internet directly — in local networks (LANs), for example. Those addresses cannot be used on the internet and are normally assigned to the devices inside an organization.\\n3. Static IP addresses:\\nStatic IP addresses are the IP addresses that are assigned to devices permanently. They do not change over time and suit servers and devices that require a constant connection.\\n4. Dynamic IP addresses:\\nDynamic IP addresses are assigned automatically, through DHCP (the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol). Those addresses can change on every connection of the device to the network, which lets you save address space and makes the configuration of the network easier.",null],[3185,"- The 400s (\`400 Bad Request\`, \`404 Not Found\`, for example) mean the problem is on the client's side (a wrong request, the absence of the page and so on).\\n\\n- The 500s (\`500 Internal Server Error\`, \`502 Bad Gateway\`, for example) mean the server was unable to handle a correct request because of its own error.","The HTTP statuses are divided into classes:\\n\\n- 4xx (client errors) – the request contains an error or cannot be performed.\\n\\n- \`400 Bad Request\` – the server did not understand the request (wrong syntax, for example).\\n\\n- \`404 Not Found\` – the resource requested has not been found.\\n\\n- \`403 Forbidden\` – the access is forbidden (there are no rights).\\n\\n- 5xx (server errors) – the server was unable to handle a correct request.\\n\\n- \`500 Internal Server Error\` – a general error of the server (a failure in the code, for example).\\n\\n- \`502 Bad Gateway\` – the proxy server received an invalid response from another server.\\n\\n- \`503 Service Unavailable\` – the server is temporarily unavailable (an overload, maintenance work).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- If the user has typed a wrong email in a form (\`400\`).\\n\\n- If the database on the server has gone down (\`500\`).",null],[3186,"CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a mechanism that permits a web page to request resources from another domain, if the server has permitted that explicitly. Without CORS the browser blocks such requests for safety.","By default browsers forbid JavaScript requests to another domain (because of the Same-Origin Policy). CORS lets you get round that restriction, if the server sends special headers — for example:\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- A simple request (a GET/POST with permitted headers):\\n\\n- The browser adds the \`Origin\` header automatically.\\n\\n- The server responds with \`Access-Control-Allow-Origin\`.\\n\\n- A preflight request (OPTIONS):\\n\\n- For \\"non-simple\\" requests (with a \`DELETE\` or with custom headers, for example) the browser first sends an \`OPTIONS\`, in order to check the permissions.\\n\\nA CORS error in the console:","Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com\\n\\nAccess to fetch at 'https://api.example.com' from origin 'https://your-site.com' has been blocked by CORS policy."],[3187,"- Cookies:\\n\\n- A small size (~4 KB per domain).\\n\\n- They are sent to the server automatically with every HTTP request.\\n\\n- They can be persistent (the lifetime expires manually) or session ones (until the browser is closed).\\n\\n- They are used for the authorization and for tracking.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- A larger size (~5 MB per domain).\\n\\n- The data is available only in the current tab and is deleted once it is closed.\\n\\n- It is not sent to the server automatically.\\n\\n- It is used for temporary data (the saving of a form, for example).","- The lifetime\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- If the \`expires\` or \`max-age\` parameter is stated, the cookies are preserved even after the browser is closed.\\n\\n- Without them they are deleted when the session ends (but the behaviour depends on the browser).\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- The data lives only while the tab is open. When it is closed, it is cleared.\\n\\n- The volume of the data\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- A limit of ~4 KB for the whole domain (all of the cookies together).\\n\\n- An overflow leads to the loss of the old cookies.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- Up to ~5 MB per domain (it depends on the browser).\\n\\n- You can hold complex data (JSON objects, for example).\\n\\n- The availability\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- They are attached to every HTTP request automatically, in the \`Cookie\` header.\\n\\n- The server can read and change them through \`Set-Cookie\`.\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- It is available only through JavaScript on the client.\\n\\n- It is not sent to the server without an explicit instruction (through \`fetch\`, for example).\\n\\n- The use\\n\\n- When to choose cookies:\\n\\n- For the authentication (a JWT, session IDs).\\n\\n- For tracking (analytics, personalization).\\n\\n- When to choose \`sessionStorage\`:\\n\\n- Temporary data within one tab (the saving of a form before its submission, for example).\\n\\n- The caching of heavy data, in order to avoid repeat computation.\\n\\n- The security\\n\\n- Cookies:\\n\\n- They are vulnerable to CSRF attacks (if there is no \`SameSite\` and \`HttpOnly\`).\\n\\n- They can be protected:\\n\\n- Session Storage:\\n\\n- It is not vulnerable to CSRF (since it is not sent automatically).\\n\\n- But it is vulnerable to XSS (if an attacker manages to run someone else's JS code).","// A cookie with a lifetime (it will be deleted in 7 days)\\ndocument.cookie = \\"token=abc123; max-age=604800; path=/\\";\\n\\n// The HTTP header from the server\\nSet-Cookie: sessionId=xyz; Secure; HttpOnly\\n\\n// An example of reading the cookies\\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // \\"token=abc123; theme=dark\\"\\n\\n// The saving of an object\\nsessionStorage.setItem('formData', JSON.stringify({ name: 'Alice', age: 25 }));\\n// The reading\\nconst data = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem('formData'));\\n\\ndocument.cookie = \\"auth=secret; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict\\";"],[3188,"CSRF attacks are possible when a site sends the credentials (the session cookies, for example) automatically without the user's confirmation. The main risks are ordinary cookies, Basic Auth and the automatic attachment of tokens to the requests.","CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) is an attack in which an attacker makes the victim's browser perform undesirable requests to a trusted site. The mechanisms that are vulnerable are the ones where:\\n\\n- Session cookies — the browser attaches them to every request automatically.\\n\\n- Basic Auth — if the login and the password are cached by the browser.\\n\\n- Automatic tokens — in forms' hidden fields without additional checks, for example.\\n\\nHow to protect yourself:\\n\\n- Use CSRF tokens (generated by the server and checked for every POST/PUT/DELETE).\\n\\n- Set the \`SameSite=Strict\` or \`Lax\` attribute on the cookies.\\n\\n- For an API, apply OAuth 2.0 with a Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE).\\n\\nAn example of a CSRF token in a form:","<form action=\\"/transfer\\" method=\\"POST\\">\\n  <input type=\\"hidden\\" name=\\"csrf_token\\" value=\\"a_random_string_from_the_server\\">\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" name=\\"amount\\">\\n</form>"],[3189,"gRPC gives high performance and the binary serialization of Protobuf, supports streaming, strict typing and the automatic generation of code, but it requires HTTP/2, is harder to debug and is not so widely supported by browsers. REST with JSON is simpler to learn, is more compatible with HTTP/1.1 and is widely supported in the tooling, but it falls behind in the speed and the completeness of the contracts.","gRPC's advantages:\\n\\n- The performance: the binary Protobuf protocol + HTTP/2.\\n\\n- Strict typing: the \`.proto\` describes the service and the messages.\\n\\n- Streaming: client and server streams are easy to implement.\\n\\n- Code generation: clients and servers for many languages.\\n\\ngRPC's disadvantages:\\n\\n- It requires HTTP/2, which is not always supported in browsers (gRPC-Web is needed).\\n\\n- The messages are less human-readable (Protobuf vs JSON).\\n\\n- It is harder to debug (special tools are needed).\\n\\nREST/JSON:\\n\\n- Broad compatibility, ease of debugging through curl or the browser.\\n\\n- It suits public APIs, where simplicity matters.\\n\\n- Larger traffic (textual JSON), no streaming by default, no automatic contract.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n\\n- Choose gRPC for highly performant microservices and for internal services.\\n\\n- REST — for public HTTP APIs and for simple integrations.",null],[3190,"The 4xx codes mean errors of the client: the request was wrong or unauthorized. The 5xx codes indicate errors of the server: an internal problem has happened on the API's or the web server's side.","The 4xx range (Client Error)\\n\\n- 400 Bad Request: incorrect syntax of the request.\\n\\n- 401 Unauthorized: the authorization token is absent or wrong.\\n\\n- 403 Forbidden: the access is forbidden, even if you are authorized.\\n\\n- 404 Not Found: the resource has not been found.\\n\\n- When to apply it: return it on wrong data from the client, or on an attempt at access without rights.\\n\\nThe 5xx range (Server Error)\\n\\n- 500 Internal Server Error: a general internal error of the server.\\n\\n- 502 Bad Gateway: an intermediate server received a wrong response.\\n\\n- 503 Service Unavailable: the service is temporarily unavailable (an overload, maintenance work).\\n\\n- 504 Gateway Timeout: an intermediate server did not receive a response in time.\\n\\n- When to apply it: use it on failures, on unforeseen exceptions or during planned maintenance.\\n\\nLet us go through the scenario:\\n\\n- The client sends wrong JSON → 400.\\n\\n- The user is unauthorized → 401.\\n\\n- The service has gone down because of an error in the code → 500.",null],[3191,"503 means the server is temporarily unable to handle the request, because of an overload or maintenance work. The clients are normally invited to retry later.","The essence of the 503 state\\n\\n- The HTTP code 503 means \\"Service Unavailable\\".\\n\\n- The server is functioning, but at the given moment it cannot handle the request.\\n\\nThe typical causes\\n\\n- An overload (a burst of traffic).\\n\\n- Planned or emergency maintenance work.\\n\\n- A limit on the resources (the CPU, the memory, the connections).\\n\\nThe recommendations for the client\\n\\n- Repeat the request after some time.\\n\\n- Use an exponential backoff (a growing interval).\\n\\nAn example of the Retry-After header\\n\\nHere the client is advised to wait 120 seconds.\\nConclusion:\\n\\n- Apply 503 when the refusal is not permanent and there are plans for a recovery.\\n\\n- For a long outage it is worth using a page with a message about the maintenance work.","HTTP/1.1 503 Service Unavailable\\nRetry-After: 120"],[3192,"HTTP transfers the data in the open, while HTTPS transfers it over a secure TLS/SSL channel. HTTPS encrypts the traffic, which protects it from being intercepted or substituted.","HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)\\n\\n- The protocol for transferring hypertext over TCP.\\n\\n- The data goes in the open.\\n\\n- It is vulnerable to \\"eavesdropping\\" and to man-in-the-middle attacks.\\n\\nHTTPS (HTTP Secure)\\n\\n- HTTP over TLS/SSL.\\n\\n- It encrypts the channel: the authentication of the server, optionally of the client.\\n\\n- It uses X.509 certificates for checking the authenticity.\\n\\nThe stages of establishing an HTTPS session\\n\\n- The client requests the server's certificate.\\n\\n- It checks the trusted certificate authority.\\n\\n- They agree on a symmetric key.\\n\\n- After that all of the traffic is encrypted.\\n\\nWhen to apply it:\\n\\n- Always for the transfer of personal data, for the authorization and for payments.\\n\\n- For public sites — by default (Let's Encrypt).",null],[3193,"GET — reading (Read); POST — creation (Create); PUT/PATCH — updating (Update); DELETE — deletion (Delete). PUT replaces the whole resource, while PATCH makes partial changes.","Create (C)\\n\\n- POST: it creates a new resource, and the URI is generated by the server.\\n\\n- An example: \`POST /users\` with a JSON body → a new user.\\n\\nRead (R)\\n\\n- GET: it requests a representation or data without a change.\\n\\n- An example: \`GET /users/123\`.\\n\\nUpdate (U)\\n\\n- PUT: the complete replacement of the resource; if there is none, it can create one.\\n\\n- PATCH: a partial change of the resource's fields.\\n\\n- An example of a PATCH: \`PATCH /users/123\` with \`{ \\"email\\": \\"new@mail\\" }\`.\\n\\nDelete (D)\\n\\n- DELETE: it deletes the resource at the URI stated.\\n\\nIdempotence\\n\\n- GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent: repeated calls give the same result.\\n\\n- POST and PATCH are not always idempotent.",null],[3194,"GET passes the parameters in the URL, which can end up in the logs and in the browser's history and can be cached. That makes the transfer of sensitive data vulnerable. It is better to use POST with a request body.","The parameters in the URL\\n\\n- All of the data is visible in the query string (\`?user=alice&token=…\`).\\n\\n- The URLs are saved in the history and in the web server's logs.\\n\\nCaching\\n\\n- Proxies and browsers can cache a GET response.\\n\\n- Potentially secret parameters are preserved in the cache.\\n\\nThe limit on the length\\n\\n- Browsers and servers limit a URL's length (normally to ~2,000 characters).\\n\\nRecommendations\\n\\n- To transfer logins, passwords and tokens, use POST or other methods with a body.\\n\\n- Always apply HTTPS for the encryption.",null],[3195,"PUT replaces the whole resource with a new representation, while PATCH changes only the fields stated. PUT is normally idempotent; PATCH can be partly idempotent, depending on the implementation.","PUT (the complete replacement)\\n\\n- The client sends the resource's complete model.\\n\\n- The fields that are absent can be reset to their default values or deleted.\\n\\n- It is idempotent: repeated calls give the same result.\\n\\nPATCH (a partial update)\\n\\n- The client sends only the fields that have changed.\\n\\n- It leaves the remaining data unchanged.\\n\\n- It can use various formats (JSON-Patch, Merge Patch).\\n\\nAn example of a JSON Merge Patch\\n\\nit replaces only the email, without touching the other fields.\\nWhen to apply them:\\n\\n- PUT — when a complete update is needed, or when creating a resource at a known URI.\\n\\n- PATCH — for saving traffic and for the convenience of partial changes.","PATCH /users/123\\nContent-Type: application/merge-patch+json\\n\\n{\\n    \\"email\\": \\"new@example.com\\"\\n}"],[3196,"Yes, according to the HTTP specification PUT can create a new resource at the URI stated, if it does not exist. That depends on the API's implementation, however, and is not always a recommendation.","The HTTP specification\\n\\n- PUT can create a resource at the target URI, if the server supports such logic.\\n\\nIdempotence\\n\\n- The first PUT creates the resource; the subsequent ones with the same data will change nothing.\\n\\nThe recommended practice\\n\\n- Create through POST, if the URI is generated by the server.\\n\\n- Use creation through PUT when the client itself determines the resource's identifier.\\n\\nAn example of creation through PUT:\\n\\nit creates a user with the ID \\"alice\\".\\nWhen to apply it:\\n\\n- When it matters to set a predictable URI.\\n\\n- For synchronizing data between distributed systems.","PUT /users/alice\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n    \\"email\\": \\"alice@example.com\\"\\n}"],[3197,"A step is added to the CI/CD pipeline, which runs the automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) on every commit or pull request. For that, CI systems (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins and others) are normally used, with configuration files in which the commands for running the tests, the installation of the dependencies and the reporting of the results are written.","The preparation of the environment:\\n\\n- You state the base image or the machine (a Docker container, a VM).\\n\\n- You install the dependencies (the libraries, the databases, the services).\\n\\nThe addition of the testing step:\\nIn the CI config (\`.gitlab-ci.yml\` or \`.github/workflows/ci.yml\`, for example) you describe a job, which performs:\\n\\n- The \`checkout\` of the code.\\n\\n- The installation of the dependencies (\`npm install\`, \`pip install -r requirements.txt\`).\\n\\n- The running of the tests (\`pytest\`, \`npm test\` and so on).\\n\\nThe reports and the artefacts:\\n\\n- The generation of the reports (JUnit, HTML, the code coverage).\\n\\n- The publication of the artefacts for an analysis of the coverage.\\n\\nParallelization and caching:\\n\\n- The splitting of the tests into several jobs, for a speed-up.\\n\\n- The caching of the dependencies between the runs.\\n\\nFail-fast and notifications:\\n\\n- The configuration of the interruption of the pipeline on failing tests (fail-fast).\\n\\n- The configuration of the notifications (email, Slack).",null],[3198,"The cookies are held in the user's browser and are sent to the server with every request. The sessions are normally held on the server (in a database or in Redis, for example), while only the session's identifier is preserved in the browser (often in a cookie).","Cookies are small text files, which are held in the browser. They are used for the authentication, for tracking users and for holding settings. When you log in to a site, for example, the server can send a cookie with an authorization token.\\nSessions are the data that is held on the server. Rather than passing all of the data back and forth, the server creates a unique session identifier (\`session_id\`, for example) and saves it in the browser's cookie. On every request the server checks that identifier and loads the session's data from its own store (a database, Redis and so on).\\nAn example of a cookie:\\n\\nAn example of a session in Redis:","Set-Cookie: session_id=abc123; Path=/; HttpOnly\\n\\n{\\n  \\"session_id\\": \\"abc123\\",\\n  \\"user_id\\": 42,\\n  \\"last_activity\\": \\"2023-10-01T12:00:00Z\\"\\n}"],[3199,"An \`OPTIONS\` request is sent by the browser automatically before some HTTP requests (a \`POST\` or a \`DELETE\` with particular headers, for example). It is part of the CORS mechanism — it checks whether the request is permitted by the server.","When the frontend and the backend are on different domains (\`frontend.com\` and \`api.backend.com\`, for example), the browser blocks \\"unsafe\\" requests for reasons of security. To make sure the server permits such requests, the browser first sends an \`OPTIONS\` (a preflight request).\\nAn example:\\n\\n- The frontend sends a \`POST /data\` with the header \`Content-Type: application/json\`.\\n\\n- The browser first sends:\\n\\n- The server responds:\\n\\n- If the server's response permits the request, the browser sends the main \`POST\`.","OPTIONS /data HTTP/1.1\\nHost: api.backend.com\\nOrigin: https://frontend.com\\nAccess-Control-Request-Method: POST\\n\\nHTTP/1.1 204 No Content\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://frontend.com\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: POST, GET"],[3200,"To obtain data in real time, people use:\\n\\n- WebSocket – a two-way connection between the client and the server.\\n\\n- Server-Sent Events (SSE) – a stream of one-way messages from the server.\\n\\n- Long polling – requests with a long wait for the response.","1. WebSocket\\nThe ws:// or wss:// (secure) protocol, which supports two-way communication.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe pros:\\n\\n- The instant transfer of the data.\\n\\n- Support for binary data.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n\\n- It is harder to configure on the server.\\n\\n2. Server-Sent Events (SSE)\\nA one-way stream of data from the server (for notifications, for example).\\nThe client's code:\\n\\nThe server (Node.js + Express):\\n\\nThe pros:\\n\\n- Ease of implementation.\\n\\n- The automatic reconnection.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n\\n- Only server → client.\\n\\n3. Long polling\\nThe client sends a request, and the server responds only when there is data.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe pros:\\n\\n- It works even without WebSocket.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n\\n- A high load on the server.","const socket = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/ws');\\n\\nsocket.onopen = () => console.log('The connection has been established');  \\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => console.log('Received:', event.data);  \\nsocket.onclose = () => console.log('The connection has been closed');  \\n\\n// The sending of the data  \\nsocket.send('Hello, server!');\\n\\nconst eventSource = new EventSource('/updates');  \\n\\neventSource.onmessage = (event) => {  \\n    console.log('A new message:', event.data);  \\n};\\n\\napp.get('/updates', (req, res) => {  \\n    res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');  \\n    setInterval(() => {  \\n        res.write(\`data: \${new Date().toISOString()}\\\\n\\\\n\`);  \\n   }, 1000);  \\n});\\n\\nfunction fetchUpdates() {  \\n    fetch('/poll')  \\n        .then(response => response.json())  \\n        .then(data => {  \\n            console.log('The data:', data);  \\n            fetchUpdates(); // A new request  \\n        });  \\n}"]]`);export{e as default};